export const LIBRARY: Record<string, string> = {
  "knowledge/orbit.md": "---\ntitle: How Orbit works\ndescription: Orbit itself: the sky, the gate and modes, profiles, helpers, the panel, missions, the library, settings and shortcuts. Read it when the user asks how to do something in Orbit.\nalways: false\n---\n\n# How Orbit works\n\nOrbit is the app the user is talking to you in. It runs on their own computer and gives you a shell and their files, with a gate in front of everything that changes something. When they ask how Orbit works, answer from this file and point to where things are on screen. Say plainly when something is not here rather than guessing.\n\n## The sky\n\nThe first screen is the Orrery: every planet is a workspace (a folder, a conversation and everything done there). Closer to the sun means used more recently, bigger means more work went into it. Sparks mean work is running there, a peach pulse means it is waiting for the user, small moons are helpers working for it. Drag to turn the sky, scroll to zoom, click a planet to fly in. At the bottom: type what you want done and press Launch; the button on its left picks the profile, the folder button picks where. Top bar: search (Ctrl K), the list of all workspaces, today's spending, settings.\n\n## The gate\n\nEvery action passes one gate, in this order:\n1. Things that only look (reading files, listing, `git status`, about 100 known programs' read-only commands) run straight away.\n2. The user's rules: \"always allow this\", \"never that\", per workspace or everywhere. Settings, Approvals holds them.\n3. The workspace's mode:\n   - **Manual**: asks the user for everything else.\n   - **Auto**: a second, cheaper model (the judge) allows the safe things and asks about the rest.\n   - **Rules**: only what a rule allows runs; the rest is refused.\n   - **Full**: decides on its own inside the workspace folder; anything outside it, or anything that escalates (sudo and similar), goes to the judge, and unsure means no.\n\nApprovals: Y allow, A always (saves a rule), N deny. Every file is saved before it changes, so the review drawer can keep or undo each file. Esc stops a run; twice also ends the shell. While a run is going, the kill switch at the bottom of the dock stops it and ends the shell.\n\n## Profiles\n\nA profile bundles how a workspace works: approval mode, tools, layout, effort, web search, preview and plan. Built in: **Pair** (side by side, you approve), **Terminal** (dense and keyboard first), **Autopilot** (say what to build and walk away), **Frontend** (live preview beside the conversation), **Research** (web search, nothing on the machine changes). Pick one at Launch, switch in the workspace header, edit or add in Settings, Profiles.\n\n## In a workspace\n\nHeader: the name (click to rename) and folder, the profile, the mode, Ori showing what is happening, and the pill with speed, context, cost, Compact and Clear. The conversation shows every action as a row that says who let it through; open a row for the output, the diff and the reason. While you work, a message the user sends joins your next step. The composer takes `/` for commands and saved prompts, files by drop or paste, Web on or off, and effort.\n\nThe dock (right edge): the shell, review changes, git (diff, commit, branches), missions, the memory shelf (pinned facts), workspace settings, and the panel buttons.\n\n## The side panel\n\n- **Preview**: the page being built, live, at any width. Comment: click anything on the page and say what to change; comments arrive together with a pinned screenshot.\n- **Docs**: Markdown documents both of you edit, with comment threads on passages.\n- **Board**: a whiteboard of notes, checklists, images, frames and arrows.\n\n## Helpers\n\nYou can hand parts of a task to helpers (`delegate`) that run at the same time. Each is a workspace of its own the user can open and message. A builder can work on its own git branch; the user then picks Merge or Discard. Helpers never get more permission than their workspace.\n\n## Missions\n\nA mission is a request that runs on a schedule or when its webhook is called, unattended. It never waits for an answer: anything a rule or the judge does not allow is refused, and the user gets a report. In the dock, Missions.\n\n## The library\n\nSettings, Library (or the book in the sky's top bar) holds skills and knowledge:\n- **Skills** are instructions for a kind of work, loaded when a task fits. Built-ins ship with Orbit; the user's own live in `~/.orbit/skills`, a project's in `.orbit/skills` or `.claude/skills`.\n- **Knowledge** is reference the user keeps for you: files in `~/.orbit/knowledge` or a project's `.orbit/knowledge`. Ones marked \"always\" are in front of you every time; the rest you read with the knowledge tool.\n- Built-ins can be switched off, or copied to edit. New ones start blank, from a template, or are written by Ori: a small model that may only search the web and read pages drafts one from a description, usually for a few cents, and the user reviews it before saving.\n\n## Memory\n\nSettings, Memory. Now and then after a run, a small model suggests up to three\nlasting facts from the conversation; they wait in the memory drawer and in\nSettings until the user keeps, edits or dismisses them. Kept memories are in\nfront of you in every workspace (not in private ones). When an answer relies\non one, end it with `[memory: <id>]` so the user sees which. The memory shelf\nin the dock holds facts pinned to one workspace only.\n\n## Knowledge folders\n\nIn a workspace's settings (the dock), the user can name folders such as\n`docs/` that are indexed on this computer and searched by the knowledge tool\nwith a query; pass a file's path as name to read it whole. Say which file an\nanswer came from.\n\n## Review, checks and replay\n\n- Review changes (dock): every file you changed, against how it was before.\n  The user keeps or undoes a whole file or one change at a time.\n- In the preview, the check button (and your page_check tool) measures speed\n  and finds accessibility problems a page shows by itself.\n- Replay (the clapperboard in the dock, or /replay) plays a finished run\n  again, helpers side by side, with every command and file change.\n- A doc can be kept as a Markdown file in the workspace folder (the file\n  button on the doc, or doc_write with file), so it can go into git.\n\n## Plugins\n\nSettings, Plugins. A plugin is a folder in `~/.orbit/plugins` with an\n`orbit-plugin.json`, written in any language. It can add tools, slash\ncommands, panels, checks before every action, settings, skills and knowledge,\nand use Orbit itself. New plugins start off. \"New plugin\" makes one from a\ntemplate, and \"Let Claude build it\" opens a workspace in its folder where you\nwrite it with the orbit-plugins skill. A plugin never gets the API key.\n\n## Your phone\n\nSettings, Your phone. The Orbit app for Android reaches this computer from\nanywhere through a relay the user runs on a small server (a one-line install),\nend to end encrypted. Pair the phone by scanning the QR code there. Approvals\nreach the phone as alerts, through the relay or Firebase.\n\n## Settings (Ctrl ,)\n\nThe API key (encrypted on this computer), models for work, the judge and titles, approvals and rules, budgets (daily and per workspace; Orbit stops before them), profiles, integrations (the programs Orbit found, MCP servers, editors, skills), the library, and the interface (theme, motion: full, calm or off).\n\n## Ori\n\nOri is Orbit's mascot, the small star in the corner. Ask Ori (or press ?) explains parts of Orbit and offers a tour of the sky or a workspace. The setup can be run again from there.\n\n## Command line\n\n`orbit` starts it and opens the browser; `orbit .` opens the current folder as a workspace; `orbit --port`, `--no-open`, `--mock` (fake model, no API calls); `orbit check` tries real work against the API in a throwaway Orbit. Config is `~/.orbit/orbit.toml`, data `~/.orbit/orbit.db`.\n",
  "skills/accessibility/SKILL.md": "---\nname: accessibility\ndescription: Use when building, fixing or auditing web UI for accessibility to WCAG 2.2 AA: semantics, keyboard, focus, contrast, forms, ARIA, motion, and testing with keyboard, screen reader and automated tools.\n---\n\n# Accessibility\n\nTarget WCAG 2.2 level AA. When a criterion matters to a decision, read its \"Understanding\" page on w3.org/WAI rather than guessing; the exact wording and exceptions matter.\n\n## Rules in priority order\n\n1. Use native HTML first. A `<button>` gives you focus, Enter and Space, role and name for free. A `<div onclick>` gives you none of them.\n2. No ARIA is better than wrong ARIA. Add ARIA only when no native element expresses the pattern, and then follow the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) pattern exactly, including its keyboard model.\n3. Everything that works with a mouse works with a keyboard, and focus is always visible.\n4. Every control has an accessible name that matches its visible label.\n5. Information is never conveyed by colour, position or sound alone.\n\n## Semantics and structure\n\n- One `<h1>` per page; headings in order without skipping levels for styling. Style with CSS, not heading level.\n- Landmarks: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>` (exactly one), `<aside>`, `<footer>`. Label repeated landmarks: `<nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\">`.\n- Links go somewhere (`<a href>`); buttons do something (`<button type=\"button\">`). Never use `<a>` without `href` as a button.\n- Lists are `<ul>`/`<ol>`, data tables use `<table>` with `<th scope>` and a `<caption>`. Do not use tables for layout.\n- Set `<html lang=\"...\">` and `lang` on passages in another language.\n- Every page has a unique, descriptive `<title>`. In single-page apps, update it on route change and move focus to the new main heading or announce the change.\n- Images: meaningful images get `alt` that conveys purpose; decorative images get `alt=\"\"`. Icon-only buttons need a name: `<button aria-label=\"Close\">` or visually hidden text.\n- Provide a \"Skip to main content\" link as the first focusable element.\n\n## Keyboard and focus\n\n- Tab order follows the visual reading order. Never use positive `tabindex`. Use `tabindex=\"0\"` only on custom widgets and `tabindex=\"-1\"` for programmatic focus targets.\n- Visible focus on everything: `:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid; outline-offset: 2px; }`. Never remove the outline without an equal replacement. Aim for 3:1 contrast against adjacent colours.\n- Focus must not be hidden by sticky headers, footers, cookie banners or chat widgets (2.4.11). Use `scroll-padding-top` equal to the sticky header height.\n- No keyboard traps. Esc closes overlays.\n- Modals: use `<dialog>` with `showModal()`. It traps focus, makes the rest inert, and closes on Esc. On open, focus the first sensible control; on close, return focus to the trigger.\n- For off-screen or hidden-but-rendered regions, use the `inert` attribute so they are not focusable.\n- Composite widgets (tabs, menus, listboxes, grids) use roving `tabindex` and arrow keys as the APG pattern specifies.\n- Anything done by dragging must also work with single clicks or taps (2.5.7): add buttons for reorder, inputs for sliders.\n\n## Visual\n\n- Text contrast 4.5:1, large text (24px, or 18.66px bold) 3:1. UI component boundaries, icons and focus indicators 3:1 against neighbours.\n- Pointer targets at least 24 by 24 CSS px or with enough spacing (2.5.8). Prefer 44px on touch.\n- Content reflows at 320 CSS px width without two-dimensional scrolling (1.4.10) and survives 200% zoom and text spacing overrides (1.4.12). Do not fix heights on text containers.\n- Links in body text are distinguishable without colour (underline them).\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: disable parallax, auto-playing animation and large transitions. Anything that moves for more than 5 seconds needs pause, stop or hide.\n- Nothing flashes more than three times per second.\n\n## Forms\n\n- Every input has a visible `<label for>`. Placeholder is not a label.\n- Group related controls with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` (radio groups, address blocks).\n- Use the right `type` and `autocomplete` tokens (`email`, `tel`, `given-name`, `current-password`, `one-time-code`).\n- Mark required fields in text, not only with colour or an asterisk; also set `required`.\n- Errors: say what is wrong and how to fix it, in text, next to the field. Link the message with `aria-describedby`, set `aria-invalid=\"true\"`, and on submit move focus to an error summary or the first invalid field. Validate on blur or submit, not on every keystroke.\n- Do not ask again for data the user already entered in the same process (3.3.7).\n- Authentication must not depend on memorising, transcribing or puzzles (3.3.8). Allow paste into password and code fields and support password managers.\n- Status messages that do not take focus (\"Saved\", \"3 results\") go in a live region: `<div role=\"status\">`. Use `role=\"alert\"` only for urgent errors. The live region must exist in the DOM before text is inserted.\n\n## ARIA, when you need it\n\n- `aria-expanded` on disclosure triggers, `aria-controls` optional, `aria-current=\"page\"` for current nav item, `aria-pressed` for toggle buttons.\n- `aria-label` overrides content; `aria-labelledby` points to visible text and is preferred.\n- `aria-hidden=\"true\"` never on focusable elements or their ancestors.\n- `role` changes semantics only. It adds no keyboard behaviour; you must implement that.\n\n## How to test\n\n1. Automated scan. Run axe-core (via `@axe-core/cli`, Playwright with `@axe-core/playwright`, or the browser extension) and Lighthouse's accessibility audit. Fix all errors. Automated tools catch roughly a third of issues; a clean scan is a start, not a pass.\n   ```\n   npx @axe-core/cli http://localhost:3000 --exit\n   ```\n2. Keyboard pass. Unplug the mouse mentally. From the address bar, Tab through the whole page: every interactive element reachable, order logical, focus always visible and never hidden, every action possible with Enter, Space, arrows and Esc, no traps. Open and close every dialog and menu and check where focus lands.\n3. Screen reader basics. Use VoiceOver (macOS, Cmd+F5), NVDA (Windows, free) or TalkBack (Android). Check: page title read on load, heading list (VO rotor, NVDA Insert+F7) makes sense alone, landmarks present, every control announces role, name and state, form errors are announced, live updates are announced.\n4. Visual checks. Zoom to 200% and 400%; set the viewport to 320px wide; enable reduced motion in OS settings; check contrast with DevTools' contrast picker; try forced colours mode (Windows High Contrast or DevTools emulation).\n5. Inspect the accessibility tree in DevTools (Accessibility pane) for any custom widget to confirm role, name and state.\n\n## Reporting findings\n\nFor each issue give: the WCAG criterion number, where (selector or component and file), what fails for whom, and the concrete fix. Rank blockers (cannot complete a task) above friction.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] axe or Lighthouse shows no errors on every changed page.\n- [ ] Full keyboard pass done; focus visible, in order, never obscured, returned after dialogs.\n- [ ] Every control has a name matching its visible label; images have correct `alt`.\n- [ ] Headings, landmarks, `lang` and `<title>` are correct.\n- [ ] Contrast meets 4.5:1 text and 3:1 non-text, in every theme.\n- [ ] Forms have labels, autocomplete, text errors linked to fields, and announced status.\n- [ ] Works at 320px and 200% zoom; reduced motion respected; targets at least 24px.\n- [ ] Any ARIA used follows an APG pattern and its keyboard model.\n- [ ] Spot-checked with a screen reader.\n",
  "skills/api-design/SKILL.md": "---\nname: api-design\ndescription: Use when asked to design, review or extend an HTTP/JSON API: endpoints, request and response shapes, errors, pagination, versioning, auth, rate limits, or its reference documentation.\n---\n\n# API Design\n\nDesign for the client developer who will read your docs once, then rely on the API behaving the same way everywhere. Consistency beats cleverness. When extending an existing API, follow its conventions even where you would choose differently.\n\n## 1. Resources and naming\n\n- Name resources as nouns, not actions: `/invoices`, `/invoices/{id}`, `/customers/{id}/invoices`.\n- Plural collection names. Lowercase, hyphenated paths: `/payment-methods`.\n- Nest at most one level; beyond that, use a filter: `/invoices?customer_id=cus_123`.\n- IDs are opaque strings. A type prefix (`inv_`, `cus_`) helps debugging. Never expose sequential database IDs if enumeration is a concern.\n- Pick one JSON field case (`snake_case` or `camelCase`) and use it everywhere, including errors and query params.\n- Timestamps in RFC 3339 / ISO 8601 UTC: `\"2026-10-23T14:05:00Z\"`. Money as integer minor units plus currency: `{\"amount\": 1999, \"currency\": \"EUR\"}`.\n- For operations that do not fit CRUD, use a sub-resource action: `POST /invoices/{id}/send`. Keep these rare.\n\n## 2. Methods and status codes\n\n| Method | Use | Idempotent | Success |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| GET | Read | Yes | 200 |\n| POST | Create, or run an action | No | 201 + `Location` header, or 200 / 202 |\n| PUT | Replace whole resource | Yes | 200 or 204 |\n| PATCH | Partial update | Not guaranteed | 200 |\n| DELETE | Remove | Yes | 204 (or 200 with body) |\n\nStatus codes to use:\n\n- `400` malformed request or validation failure. `422` if you separate syntax errors from semantic ones; pick one convention.\n- `401` missing or invalid credentials. `403` authenticated but not allowed. `404` not found (also for resources the caller may not know exist).\n- `409` conflict (duplicate, version mismatch). `412` failed `If-Match` precondition.\n- `429` rate limited, with `Retry-After`.\n- `500` unexpected server error, `503` temporarily unavailable, with `Retry-After` when known.\n- `202` accepted for async work, returning a job resource the client can poll.\n\nNever return `200` with an error in the body.\n\n## 3. Errors with one shape\n\nUse Problem Details (RFC 9457, `application/problem+json`) or one equivalent shape for every error:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"https://api.example.com/errors/validation\",\n  \"title\": \"Invalid request\",\n  \"status\": 422,\n  \"detail\": \"2 fields failed validation.\",\n  \"instance\": \"/invoices\",\n  \"request_id\": \"req_8f2c\",\n  \"errors\": [\n    { \"field\": \"due_date\", \"code\": \"in_past\", \"message\": \"Must be today or later.\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- A stable machine-readable `type` or `code`; clients branch on it, never on `message`.\n- Field-level errors point at the field path.\n- Include a request ID in every response (body and header) for support.\n- Never leak stack traces, SQL, or internal hostnames.\n\n## 4. Pagination, filtering, sorting\n\n- Paginate every list endpoint from day one. Adding it later is a breaking change.\n- Prefer cursor pagination: `GET /invoices?limit=50&cursor=abc`, response `{\"data\": [...], \"next_cursor\": \"def\"}` (`null` at the end). Offset pagination is acceptable for small, stable, admin-style lists.\n- Default and maximum `limit` documented (for example 20 and 100).\n- Cursors are opaque; sort order must be stable (tie-break on ID).\n- Filters as query params with exact names: `?status=paid&created_after=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z`.\n- Sorting: `?sort=-created_at` (minus for descending). Allow only indexed fields.\n\n## 5. Idempotency and concurrency\n\n- Accept an `Idempotency-Key` header on POST (and PATCH where retries matter). Store key, request fingerprint, and response for a documented window (often 24 hours).\n- Same key, same body: return the stored response. Same key, different body: `422` or `409`. Same key still in flight: `409`.\n- For lost updates, return an `ETag` and require `If-Match` on writes where concurrent edits are plausible.\n\n## 6. Versioning and change\n\n- Additive changes are non-breaking: new endpoints, new optional fields, new enum values (only if clients were told to tolerate unknown values).\n- Breaking: removing or renaming fields, changing types or meaning, new required inputs, changed defaults, stricter validation.\n- Version in the path (`/v1/`) for simplicity, or a header/date-based version if the API already does that. Do not mix.\n- Deprecate before removing: announce, add a `Deprecation` and `Sunset` header, give a date, and keep the old behavior until then.\n\n## 7. Auth and security\n\n- HTTPS only. Reject plain HTTP rather than redirecting API calls.\n- Server-to-server: API keys or OAuth 2.0 client credentials in the `Authorization` header, never in query strings. User-delegated: OAuth 2.0 authorization code with PKCE.\n- Scope keys to least privilege; support rotation (two active keys at once).\n- Check authorization per object on every request, not just per route. Return `404` rather than `403` when revealing existence would leak information.\n- Validate and bound every input: size limits, enum checks, max array lengths.\n\n## 8. Rate limits\n\n- Limit per key (and per IP for unauthenticated routes). Return `429` with `Retry-After`.\n- Expose remaining quota in response headers. Follow the IETF RateLimit header fields if the stack supports them, or the API's existing `X-RateLimit-*` convention; document whichever you use.\n- Document the limits and tell clients to back off exponentially with jitter.\n\n## 9. Document with examples\n\nFor every endpoint document: method and path, purpose in one line, auth and scopes, path/query/body params (type, required, default, constraints), a full example request (`curl`) and response, every error `type` it can return, and pagination or idempotency behavior.\n\nWrite or update an OpenAPI description alongside the design. Keep examples real: they should run against a test environment.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Resource names are plural nouns, paths and fields follow one casing convention.\n- [ ] Every method/status pair matches the table; no `200` errors.\n- [ ] All errors share one shape with a stable code and request ID.\n- [ ] Every list endpoint paginates with documented defaults and limits.\n- [ ] POST endpoints that create or charge accept an idempotency key.\n- [ ] The change is classified as breaking or non-breaking, and breaking changes are versioned or deprecated with a date.\n- [ ] Auth is in headers, per-object authorization is checked, inputs are bounded.\n- [ ] Rate limit responses include `429` and `Retry-After`.\n- [ ] Each endpoint has a working request and response example and an OpenAPI entry.\n",
  "skills/code-review/SKILL.md": "---\nname: code-review\ndescription: Use when reviewing a diff, pull request, branch or existing codebase: finding real defects in order of severity, verifying them by running code, and writing findings with concrete failure scenarios.\n---\n\n# Code Review\n\nThe goal is to find what will break, leak, corrupt or mislead, and to say so precisely. A review with three proven bugs beats one with thirty style remarks.\n\n## Set up\n\n1. Establish scope: which commits, files or modules. For a branch: `git diff --stat main...HEAD`, then `git log --oneline main..HEAD`.\n2. Read the description, linked issue and tests first to learn what the change claims to do. Review against that intent.\n3. Read the full diff, then the surrounding code of every changed function. Bugs often live in what the change did not touch: other callers, the other branch of a condition, the matching cleanup.\n4. Find the callers of changed functions (`grep -rn \"functionName(\" src/`) and check each still works with the new behaviour.\n5. Install and run the project's own checks: build, type check, linter, tests. Note what fails before you start forming opinions.\n\n## What to look for, in order of severity\n\n1. Security: injection (SQL, shell, path traversal, HTML or template), missing authentication or authorisation checks, secrets in code or logs, unsafe deserialisation, SSRF, insecure defaults, user input reaching `eval`, `exec` or a shell.\n2. Data loss and corruption: missing transactions, partial writes, races on shared state, wrong migrations, destructive operations without guards, lost updates, incorrect cache invalidation.\n3. Correctness: logic errors, off-by-one, wrong conditions, unhandled null or empty, wrong units or time zones, integer overflow, floating point money, error paths that swallow or mis-handle failures, promises not awaited, resources not closed.\n4. Concurrency and lifecycle: shared mutable state, re-entrancy, retries without idempotency, timeouts missing, event listeners and subscriptions not removed.\n5. Contract and compatibility: public API or schema changes that break callers, changed defaults, config or env vars renamed without migration.\n6. Performance with real consequence: N+1 queries, unbounded loops or memory over user-controlled size, blocking calls on hot paths, missing pagination.\n7. Tests: does a test fail if the change is reverted? Are edge cases and error paths covered? Do tests assert behaviour, not implementation details? Are any tests skipped or weakened?\n8. Design and maintainability: unnecessary complexity, duplication of existing helpers, misleading names, dead code, comments that contradict the code.\n\nStop going deeper on lower categories while higher ones have unresolved findings.\n\n## Verify, do not speculate\n\nA finding you have not checked is a question, not a finding.\n- Reproduce suspected bugs: write a small failing test or a script that calls the function with the problematic input, and run it.\n  ```\n  node -e \"const {parse} = require('./dist/parse'); console.log(parse(''))\"\n  python -c \"from app.dates import to_utc; print(to_utc('2026-03-29T02:30', 'Europe/Berlin'))\"\n  ```\n- Check claims in the description: if it says \"handles empty input\", try empty input.\n- Before saying \"this is never called\" or \"this is unused\", search for it, including dynamic access and config.\n- Before saying \"this library does X\", read its docs or source for the version in the lockfile.\n- If you cannot verify (needs production data, external service), say so and label the finding as unverified with what would confirm it.\n\n## Writing findings\n\nEach finding has:\n- Severity: blocker, major, minor, or nit.\n- Location: `path/to/file.ts:142` (and the function name).\n- What happens: the concrete failure scenario. Input or sequence of events, then the wrong result. \"If two requests update the same order concurrently, the second `save()` overwrites the first's `status`, because the read and write are not in a transaction.\"\n- Evidence: the test, command or output that shows it, or the exact lines that prove it.\n- Fix: a specific suggestion, with a short code snippet if it helps. Offer it, do not rewrite the change.\n\nExample:\n```\n[major] src/billing/invoice.ts:88 computeTotal()\nA fixed discount is subtracted after tax, so a 100.00 item with 20% tax and a 10.00 voucher\ntotals 110.00; docs/billing.md says discounts apply to the subtotal, giving 108.00.\nVerified: added test `applies voucher before tax`, fails with expected 108.00, received 110.00.\nFix: subtract the discount from the subtotal before calling applyTax().\n```\nKeep findings factual; if the expected behaviour is a policy question, ask it explicitly rather than asserting.\n\nOrder findings by severity. Start the review with a two or three line summary: overall verdict (approve, approve with fixes, request changes), the most important issue, and what you verified by running.\n\n## What not to do\n\n- Do not nitpick formatting that a formatter or linter handles. If the project has no formatter, mention it once.\n- Do not demand personal style preferences, renames for taste, or rewrites of code the change did not touch.\n- Do not pad the review with praise or with generic advice (\"consider adding more tests\").\n- Do not report the same pattern ten times; report it once and list the locations.\n- Do not mark something a bug because it is unfamiliar. Check first.\n- Do not block on nits. Label them `nit:` so the author can ignore them.\n- Do not change the code under review unless asked; the deliverable is the review.\n\n## Reviewing a whole codebase\n\n- Start with the entry points, request handlers, auth, data access and anything that touches files, shell or network.\n- Run the test suite and note coverage gaps in risky areas.\n- Search for known hazard patterns: `eval(`, `exec(`, `shell=True`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, string-built SQL, `TODO`, `FIXME`, `catch {}` or `except: pass`, hardcoded credentials.\n- Check dependency health with the ecosystem's audit tool (`npm audit`, `pip-audit`, `cargo audit`) and report only issues that are reachable.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Full diff read, plus callers and surrounding code of every changed function.\n- [ ] Build, type check, lint and tests run; results stated.\n- [ ] Security, data integrity and correctness checked before anything else.\n- [ ] Every blocker and major finding verified by a test, script or exact code citation, or marked unverified.\n- [ ] Each finding has severity, file and line, failure scenario and a fix.\n- [ ] No style nits that tooling covers; nits labelled as nits.\n- [ ] Summary with verdict at the top.\n",
  "skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md": "---\nname: data-analysis\ndescription: Use when asked to analyse a CSV, spreadsheet, JSON file or database export: answering questions about the data, finding trends, summarising, comparing groups, or charting it.\n---\n\n# Data Analysis\n\nEvery number you report comes from code you ran on the data. You never estimate, round from memory, or eyeball a figure in prose.\n\n## 1. Clarify the question\n\n- Restate the question as something computable: \"Which region grew revenue fastest in 2025?\" becomes \"sum of `amount` by `region` and quarter, growth Q1 to Q4\".\n- Note what you need to define: what counts as a customer, active, revenue (gross or net), the time window, the time zone.\n- If a definition changes the answer materially and you cannot infer it, ask. Otherwise pick the most reasonable one and state it.\n\n## 2. Load and inspect before analysing\n\nWork in a script (Python with pandas, or DuckDB / SQLite for large files or SQL-shaped questions). Save it in the working folder so it can be rerun.\n\nInspect first, and look at the output:\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nprint(df.shape)\nprint(df.dtypes)\nprint(df.head(10))\nprint(df.isna().sum())\nprint(df.describe(include=\"all\").T)\nprint(df.duplicated().sum())\n```\n\nCheck, and write down what you find:\n\n- **Shape**: row count matches what the user expects? A file truncated at 65,536 or 1,048,576 rows came from a spreadsheet limit.\n- **Header and encoding**: the real header row, extra title rows, footer totals, a BOM, `;` vs `,` separators, decimal commas.\n- **Types**: numbers stored as text (`\"1,234\"`, `\"$5\"`, `\"12%\"`), dates as strings, IDs read as numbers (leading zeros lost).\n- **Missing values**: count per column, and the disguised ones: `\"\"`, `\"N/A\"`, `\"-\"`, `0`, `1900-01-01`, `9999`.\n- **Duplicates**: exact duplicate rows and duplicate keys (same order ID twice).\n- **Ranges and categories**: negative quantities, future dates, outliers, inconsistent labels (`\"UK\"`, `\"U.K.\"`, `\"United Kingdom\"`).\n- **Grain**: what one row represents (an order, an order line, a daily snapshot). Summing snapshots double counts.\n\nFor spreadsheets, list the sheets first and check for merged cells, hidden rows, and formulas whose cached values may be stale.\n\n## 3. Clean explicitly\n\n- Do each fix in code, with a comment, and print how many rows it touched.\n- Never silently drop rows. Report: \"Dropped 37 rows with no `order_id` (0.4%).\"\n- Keep the raw data unchanged; write cleaned output to a new file if you save it.\n- Parse dates with an explicit format and time zone.\n\n## 4. Compute in code\n\n- Every figure in your answer is printed by the script. Copy numbers from the output, do not retype or recompute them mentally.\n- Prefer simple, explainable methods: counts, sums, means and medians, group-bys, percentages, year-over-year change. Use medians for skewed values like income, latency, order size.\n- Report the denominator with every percentage: \"18% (412 of 2,290 orders)\".\n- For comparisons between groups, show group sizes. Flag groups too small to support a conclusion.\n- Do not claim causation from observational data. Say \"associated with\".\n- If you use a statistical test or regression, say which one, why, and what its assumptions are, in one or two lines.\n\n## 5. Check your totals\n\nBefore reporting:\n\n- Sum of group totals equals the overall total.\n- Row counts before and after each join and filter are what you expect. A join that increases rows means duplicate keys.\n- Spot-check two or three individual records by hand against the raw file.\n- If the data includes its own totals (a summary row, a figure the user quoted), reconcile with it and explain any difference.\n- Sanity-check magnitude: a 4,000% growth rate or an average age of 212 is a data problem until proven otherwise.\n\n## 6. Chart only when it helps\n\n- Line for change over time. Bar (horizontal if labels are long) for comparing categories. Histogram or box plot for distributions. Scatter for the relationship between two numeric variables.\n- Avoid pie charts beyond three slices, 3D, dual y-axes, and stacked bars with many segments.\n- Bars start at zero. Label axes with units. Title states the finding: \"Returns doubled after the March price change\", not \"Returns by month\".\n- Sort categorical bars by value unless the categories have a natural order.\n- Save charts as PNG files next to the script and reference them in the answer. Keep to one to three charts.\n\n## 7. Deliver\n\nShape the answer like this:\n\n```markdown\n**Answer:** The North region grew fastest: revenue up 31% from Q1 to Q4 2025 ($1.20M to $1.57M).\n\n- West: +12%, South: +4%, East: -3%.\n- North's growth came mostly from two new accounts (62% of the increase).\n\n**Assumptions:** revenue is `amount` net of refunds; dates in UTC; 37 rows without region excluded.\n**Data issues:** 12 duplicate order IDs removed; `discount` is empty before March.\n**Files:** `analysis.py` (rerun with `python analysis.py`), `growth_by_region.png`.\n```\n\n- Lead with the direct answer and its key number.\n- Then two to five supporting points.\n- Then assumptions, data quality caveats, and the files.\n- Include the code or point to the saved script so the result can be reproduced.\n- Round sensibly for reading (two or three significant figures), but keep exact values in the output tables.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] You inspected shape, types, missing values, duplicates, and grain before analysing.\n- [ ] Every cleaning step is in code and its row impact is reported.\n- [ ] Every number in the answer appears in the script's output.\n- [ ] Group totals reconcile with the overall total; joins did not inflate rows.\n- [ ] Percentages carry denominators; small groups are flagged.\n- [ ] Charts have labelled axes with units, zero-based bars, and a finding as the title.\n- [ ] Assumptions and definitions are stated.\n- [ ] The script is saved and reruns from scratch.\n- [ ] The answer opens with the direct answer to the question asked.\n",
  "skills/debugging/SKILL.md": "---\nname: debugging\ndescription: Use when something fails, crashes, hangs or gives wrong results and the cause is not obvious: reproduce, narrow, form and test hypotheses one at a time, bisect, fix the root cause and add a regression test.\n---\n\n# Debugging\n\nWork like an experimenter: observe, form one hypothesis, run one test that can disprove it, record the result. Never change code at random hoping the symptom goes away.\n\n## 1. Read the actual error\n\n- Read the full error message and the whole stack trace, top to bottom. Find the first frame in project code, not in the library.\n- Read the log lines before the error; the first failure often causes the later ones. Look for the earliest anomaly, not the loudest.\n- Copy exact text. Do not paraphrase error messages in your notes; search the codebase and dependency source for the exact string.\n- Check exit codes and stderr, not just stdout: `cmd; echo \"exit=$?\"`.\n- If there is no error, define the wrong behaviour precisely: input, expected output, actual output.\n\n## 2. Reproduce\n\n- Get a command that shows the failure every time. Write it down.\n  ```\n  npm test -- -t \"parses empty header\"\n  pytest tests/test_dates.py::test_dst -x -vv\n  curl -sS -X POST localhost:8080/api/orders -d @payload.json\n  ```\n- If it is intermittent, make it frequent: loop it (`for i in $(seq 50); do cmd || break; done`), add load, shrink timeouts, fix the random seed, run tests in random order or in isolation.\n- Record the environment: versions of runtime and key dependencies, OS, config, env vars, data. \"Works here, fails there\" is a difference in one of these; diff them.\n- You cannot claim a fix for a bug you cannot reproduce. If reproduction fails, add logging that will capture the next occurrence and say so.\n\n## 3. Narrow\n\nShrink the search space before theorising.\n- Minimise the input: cut the payload, file or test case in half repeatedly until the smallest version still fails.\n- Minimise the code path: call the failing function directly from a small script or test, bypassing the UI and network.\n- Find the boundary: the last point where data is correct and the first point where it is wrong. Log or assert at the midpoint and halve again.\n- Check assumptions at boundaries: what is actually passed in, what is actually returned, which config file was actually loaded, which version of a module is actually imported (`node -p \"require.resolve('pkg')\"`, `python -c \"import pkg; print(pkg.__file__, pkg.__version__)\"`).\n\n### Bisect when it used to work\n\nIf a known-good version exists, let git find the change:\n```\ngit bisect start\ngit bisect bad HEAD\ngit bisect good v1.4.0\ngit bisect run ./repro.sh   # exit 0 = good, 1 to 127 = bad, 125 = skip (cannot test)\ngit bisect reset\n```\nMake `repro.sh` deterministic and fast. The same halving works on dependency versions, config changes and data: revert half, test, repeat.\n\n## 4. Hypothesise and test one thing at a time\n\n- Write the hypothesis as a falsifiable statement: \"The cache returns a stale user because the key omits tenant ID.\" Then name the observation that would prove it wrong.\n- Change one variable per experiment. If you change two things and it works, you do not know why.\n- Prefer observation over modification: add a log line, a breakpoint or an assertion before editing logic.\n- Use the debugger when state is complex: `node --inspect-brk`, `python -m pdb` or `breakpoint()`, `dlv debug`, `gdb`/`lldb`. Inspect actual values; do not infer them.\n- Revert every experimental change that did not help before trying the next one. Keep the tree clean between experiments (`git stash` or `git diff` to check).\n- If three hypotheses fail, step back: re-read the error, question the assumption you have not tested, and check the environment again.\n\n## 5. Keep a log of what you ruled out\n\nKeep short notes as you go:\n```\nsymptom: POST /orders returns 500 only for EUR orders\nrepro: ./repro.sh (fails 10/10)\nruled out: DB connection (other queries fine), payload size (minimal payload fails), currency table (rows present)\nhypothesis: rounding in toMinorUnits() throws for 3-decimal rates -> testing with rate 1.085\n```\nThis prevents retesting the same idea and is what you report if you must stop before the root cause.\n\n## Common causes to check\n\n- Off-by-one, null or undefined, empty collection, wrong type coercion.\n- Time zones, DST, locale, encoding (UTF-8 vs Latin-1, BOM, line endings).\n- Caching at any layer: build cache, browser, CDN, memoisation, stale compiled output. Clean build and retry.\n- Wrong file or wrong version actually running: stale process, old container, path shadowing, duplicate dependency.\n- Async ordering: missing `await`, race between handlers, unhandled rejection, timers.\n- Environment: missing env var, different config file, permissions, ports in use, disk full.\n- State leaking between tests: shared fixtures, global singletons, test order.\n\n## 6. Fix the root cause\n\n- Fix where the wrong value originates, not where it is noticed. A null check at the crash site that hides bad upstream data is a second bug.\n- Ask whether the same mistake exists elsewhere: search for the pattern and fix or report the siblings.\n- Keep the fix minimal and separate from refactoring.\n\n## 7. Prove it\n\n- Write a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after. Run it both ways to confirm.\n- Run the original reproduction command; for intermittent bugs, run the loop enough times to be convincing.\n- Run the full relevant test suite to check nothing else broke.\n- Remove temporary logging, breakpoints and debug flags.\n\n## Report\n\nState: the symptom, the root cause (with file and line), why it happened, the fix, the regression test, and what was ruled out. If unresolved, state the reproduction, what was ruled out, the current best hypothesis and the next experiment.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] The failure was reproduced with a written-down command.\n- [ ] The root cause is identified and explained, not just the symptom suppressed.\n- [ ] A regression test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n- [ ] The original reproduction now passes, repeatedly if the bug was intermittent.\n- [ ] Full relevant test suite passes.\n- [ ] Experimental changes and debug output are removed.\n- [ ] Notes record what was ruled out.\n",
  "skills/dependency-upgrades/SKILL.md": "---\nname: dependency-upgrades\ndescription: Use when upgrading a library, framework, toolchain or language runtime, especially across a major version, or when fixing breakage caused by an upgrade or a lockfile change.\n---\n\n# Dependency Upgrades\n\nUpgrade one thing at a time, on a branch, with the changelog open, and fix each breakage at its cause.\n\n## Establish the starting point\n\n1. Confirm the working tree is clean and create a branch: `git switch -c upgrade/<package>-<target>`.\n2. Run the full build, test suite, linter and type checker. Record the baseline. Pre-existing failures are not yours to explain later.\n3. Find the current version actually installed, not just the declared range:\n   - `npm ls <pkg>`, `pnpm why <pkg>`, `yarn why <pkg>`\n   - `pip show <pkg>`, `uv tree`, `poetry show <pkg>`\n   - `cargo tree -i <pkg>`, `go list -m all | grep <pkg>`, `bundle info <pkg>`\n4. Find who depends on it transitively; another package may pin it or require a range.\n\n## Read before you change\n\n- Read the official changelog and migration or upgrade guide for every major version between current and target. Skipping one means missing its breaking changes.\n- Check the target's supported runtime versions and peer dependencies. A library upgrade may force a runtime or framework upgrade first.\n- Look for an official codemod or migration tool.\n- Search the project's issue tracker for known regressions in the target release. Prefer the latest patch of the target line.\n- Write down the breaking changes that could affect this codebase. That list drives the rest of the work.\n\nDo not rely on memory for what changed between versions. Check the current docs.\n\n## Find every use\n\n```sh\nrg -n \"from ['\\\"]<pkg>|require\\(['\\\"]<pkg>|import <pkg>|<pkg>::\" --hidden -g '!node_modules' -g '!vendor'\n```\n\n- Include config files, build scripts, Dockerfiles, CI images, `.tool-versions`, `.nvmrc`, `engines`, `python_requires`, `rust-toolchain`, `go` directives.\n- Cross-check each item on your breaking-change list against these uses. Mark each as affected or not.\n\n## Upgrade in steps\n\n- Upgrade one major version at a time when the gap spans several and each has its own migration guide.\n- Upgrade one package or tightly coupled group at a time (a framework and its official plugins move together). Never bundle unrelated upgrades.\n- Use the package manager to change versions so the lockfile is regenerated consistently:\n  - `npm install <pkg>@<version>`, `pnpm up <pkg>@<version>`, `yarn up <pkg>@<version>`\n  - `uv add <pkg>==<version>` or `poetry add <pkg>@<version>` or edit requirements and recompile the lock\n  - `cargo update -p <pkg> --precise <version>` or edit `Cargo.toml`, `go get <module>@<version>`, `bundle update <gem> --conservative`\n- Run the official codemod if one exists, then read its diff. Codemods miss dynamic usages and sometimes produce code that compiles but behaves differently.\n- Commit the version bump plus lockfile, then the code changes it required, in reviewable steps.\n\n## Lockfiles\n\n- Commit lockfiles for applications. Never delete and regenerate a lockfile to resolve a conflict or an error; that silently upgrades everything else.\n- On a lockfile merge conflict, take one side, then rerun the install command to reconcile.\n- In CI and for verification, install exactly from the lock: `npm ci`, `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, `yarn install --immutable`, `uv sync --locked`, `cargo build --locked`, `bundle install` with frozen settings.\n- Review the lockfile diff: unexpected new packages or many unrelated version changes mean something went wrong.\n\n## Fix by cause, not symptom\n\nWhen something breaks:\n1. Read the full error and find the first failure.\n2. Match it against the breaking-change list and the migration guide. Most failures are documented.\n3. Apply the documented replacement API or setting.\n\nDo not:\n- Pin a transitive dependency to an old version to silence an error without understanding why.\n- Add type casts, `any`, `# type: ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` or lint disables to get past new type errors.\n- Wrap new exceptions in broad catches.\n- Downgrade back silently. If the upgrade is blocked, stop and report why.\n\nDeprecation warnings that appear after the upgrade are the next major's breaking changes. Fix them now if cheap, otherwise list them.\n\n## Verify behaviour, not just the build\n\n- Run the full test suite, type checker, linter and production build.\n- Behavioural changes often pass tests: default values, timezone or locale handling, serialization formats, sort stability, HTTP client timeouts, retry defaults, logging format. Check each documented default change against your usage.\n- Start the application and exercise the paths that use the dependency most.\n- For runtime upgrades, also check: native extensions rebuild, Docker base images, CI matrix versions, and deployment environment versions all match.\n- Run the ecosystem's vulnerability audit after the upgrade.\n\n## Report\n\nState: from and to versions, breaking changes that applied and how you handled each, codemods run, anything pinned or deferred and why, and any behavioural difference a user might notice.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Branch created; baseline results recorded.\n- [ ] Changelog and migration guide read for every major version crossed.\n- [ ] Every use found and checked against the breaking-change list.\n- [ ] Version changed through the package manager; lockfile regenerated, reviewed and committed.\n- [ ] Codemod output reviewed by hand.\n- [ ] Breakages fixed at their cause; no suppressions, no stray pins.\n- [ ] Tests, types, lint, build and a manual run pass; frozen-lockfile install works.\n- [ ] Runtime, CI and container versions consistent.\n- [ ] Summary lists versions, changes made and anything deferred.\n",
  "skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md": "---\nname: frontend-design\ndescription: Use when building or reshaping a web page or app UI and the result should look intentional rather than templated: typography, colour, spacing, layout, states, motion and responsive behaviour.\n---\n\n# Frontend Design\n\n## Decide the direction before writing CSS\n\n1. Read what exists. If the project has a design system, tokens, a component library or a brand guide, follow it and extend it. Do not invent a parallel style.\n2. Name the job of the screen in one sentence and the one action that matters most. Everything on the page is ranked against that.\n3. Pick one clear aesthetic direction and write it down in three to five words (for example \"dense, technical, monochrome with one signal colour\" or \"warm editorial, generous whitespace, serif headings\"). Every later choice should be traceable to it.\n4. Decide what will be memorable: one distinctive element (a type pairing, an unusual grid, a strong colour field, a custom illustration style). One is enough. Five is noise.\n\n## Avoid the templated defaults\n\nThese read as \"nobody decided anything\". Do not reach for them unless the context calls for them:\n- Purple to blue gradients on white, gradient text in hero headings.\n- Inter or the system font for everything, all at weights 400 and 600.\n- Three equal feature cards with an icon, title and two lines of text.\n- Every container a rounded card with the same shadow; radius 8 to 16px on everything.\n- Centred hero, big headline, two buttons, screenshot below.\n- Emoji as icons, decorative blobs, glassmorphism with no purpose.\n- Grey text on light grey backgrounds to look \"subtle\".\n\nReplacing a default needs a reason tied to the direction, not novelty for its own sake.\n\n## Typography\n\n- Choose at most two families: one for display or headings, one for text and UI. A monospace for code or data is a third only when it is needed.\n- Pair on contrast (serif with grotesque, condensed with wide), not on near-duplicates.\n- Build a type scale with a fixed ratio (1.2 for dense apps, 1.25 to 1.333 for content, larger for editorial). Use 5 to 7 steps; do not freehand sizes.\n- Use fluid sizes for headings: `font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.2rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);`. Keep body text in `rem` so it respects user zoom and settings.\n- Body text: 16px minimum, line height 1.4 to 1.6, measure 45 to 75 characters (`max-width: 65ch`). Headings: tighter line height (1.05 to 1.25) and slightly negative tracking at large sizes.\n- Use weight, size and colour for hierarchy, in that order of preference. Avoid more than three weights.\n- Use `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` for tables, prices and counters.\n- Load web fonts with `font-display: swap`, subset them, and prefer variable fonts when you need several weights.\n\n## Colour\n\n- Define colours as tokens on `:root` (`--bg`, `--surface`, `--text`, `--text-muted`, `--border`, `--accent`, `--danger`, `--success`). Never hardcode hex values in components.\n- Start from neutrals with a slight hue tint, then add one accent. Add a second accent only with a defined role.\n- Use `oklch()` for palettes so lightness steps are perceptually even; generate a ramp (for example 10 steps) per hue rather than picking shades by eye.\n- Reserve the accent for the primary action, selection and focus. If everything is accented, nothing is.\n- Check contrast as you go: 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for large text, icons and control borders.\n- Support dark mode by redefining tokens under `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`, not by inverting. Dark surfaces are dark grey, not pure black; reduce accent saturation slightly; express elevation with lighter surfaces instead of shadows.\n\n## Spacing and layout\n\n- Use a spacing scale on a 4px or 8px base (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96). Every margin, padding and gap comes from it.\n- Group by proximity: space inside a group is clearly smaller than space between groups.\n- Use CSS Grid for page structure and Flexbox for one-dimensional rows. Prefer `gap` over margins between siblings.\n- Use container queries (`container-type: inline-size; @container (min-width: 30rem)`) for components that live in different widths, and media queries for page-level layout.\n- Align to a grid. Break it deliberately once, where you want attention.\n- Let content set widths: `max-width` on text, `minmax()` and `auto-fit` for card grids: `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));`.\n\n## Components and states\n\nDesign every interactive element in all its states, not just the resting one:\n- Default, hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading.\n- Selected or current (nav item, tab, row).\n- Empty, loading (skeleton matching the final layout), error with a way forward, partial data, very long content, zero results.\n\nRules:\n- Focus styles are designed, visible and high contrast: `:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }`. Never `outline: none` without a replacement.\n- One primary button per view. Secondary actions are visibly quieter.\n- Buttons say what they do (\"Save changes\", not \"Submit\"). Destructive actions look different and ask for confirmation or offer undo.\n- Hit targets at least 24px, ideally 44px on touch.\n- Test with real or realistic data: long names, missing images, 0 and 10,000 items, other languages.\n\n## Motion\n\n- Motion explains change: where something came from, what changed, what is loading. No motion for decoration alone.\n- Durations: 100 to 200ms for small UI feedback, 200 to 350ms for panels and page transitions. Ease out when entering, ease in when leaving.\n- Animate `transform` and `opacity` only. Do not animate `width`, `height`, `top` or `left`.\n- For page or state transitions, consider the View Transitions API where supported, with a plain fallback.\n- Wrap non-essential motion: `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { *, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; scroll-behavior: auto !important; } }`.\n\n## Responsive\n\n- Design mobile first, then add layout as space allows. Check at 320, 375, 768, 1024, 1440px and at an odd width in between.\n- No horizontal scroll at 320px. Tables either scroll inside their own container or reflow.\n- Respect safe areas on phones (`env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`) for fixed bars.\n- Images: `max-width: 100%; height: auto;`, explicit `width` and `height` attributes, `srcset` and `sizes` for photos.\n- Check at 200% browser zoom and with a larger default font size.\n\n## Working method\n\n1. Build tokens first (type scale, spacing, colours, radii, shadows), then layout, then components.\n2. Render the page and look at it. Take a screenshot at mobile and desktop widths if you can and judge it as a user would: what is seen first, second, third.\n3. Squint test: blur your eyes at the screenshot. The primary action and heading must still stand out.\n4. Remove one thing. Most first drafts have one decoration, border or colour too many.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Direction is written down and every major choice follows it.\n- [ ] No templated defaults left in without a reason.\n- [ ] Type, spacing and colour all come from tokens and scales.\n- [ ] All interactive states exist, including focus-visible, disabled, loading, empty and error.\n- [ ] Text contrast meets 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text and UI parts) in both light and dark.\n- [ ] Works at 320px wide with no horizontal scroll, and at 200% zoom.\n- [ ] Motion uses transform and opacity and respects `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- [ ] Tested with realistic, long and empty data.\n- [ ] You looked at the rendered result, not just the code.\n",
  "skills/git-workflow/SKILL.md": "---\nname: git-workflow\ndescription: Use when committing, branching, rebasing, merging or resolving conflicts in a git repository, or when cleaning up history before sharing work.\n---\n\n# Git Workflow\n\nLeave history that a reviewer can read and a bisect can trust: each commit is one logical change, builds, and passes tests.\n\n## Look before you act\n\nRun these before any commit, rebase or push:\n\n```sh\ngit status\ngit branch --show-current\ngit log --oneline -10\ngit remote -v\ngit stash list\n```\n\nKnow which branch you are on, whether it tracks a remote, and whether there is uncommitted work that is not yours. Never discard or overwrite changes you did not make (`git checkout -- .`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd`) without the user's say-so.\n\n## Branches\n\n- Do not commit directly to `main`, `master` or a release branch unless the user asks. Create a branch: `git switch -c fix/login-timeout`.\n- Name branches by intent: `fix/...`, `feat/...`, `refactor/...`, `chore/...`, or the project's own convention if it has one.\n- Start from an up-to-date base: `git fetch origin && git switch -c <name> origin/main`.\n- Keep a branch to one topic. Unrelated fixes go on their own branch.\n\n## Commits in steps that each build\n\n- Stage deliberately. Use `git add -p` or name paths. Avoid `git add -A` and `git add .` unless you have reviewed `git status` and every file belongs.\n- One logical change per commit: a rename, then the behaviour change that uses it, then the test update. Not \"misc fixes\".\n- Each commit must build and pass tests on its own. If an intermediate step would break the build, combine it with the step that fixes it.\n- Formatting and whitespace changes go in a separate commit from logic changes.\n- Generated files and lockfiles go in the same commit as the change that produced them.\n\n## Commit messages\n\n```\nReject uploads larger than the configured limit\n\nThe handler buffered the whole body before checking size, so a large\nupload could exhaust memory. Check Content-Length first and stream with\na hard cap for chunked requests.\n\nFixes #482\n```\n\n- Subject: imperative mood (\"Add\", \"Fix\", \"Remove\", not \"Added\" or \"Fixes\"), about 50 characters, at most 72, no trailing period, capitalised unless the project's convention says otherwise.\n- Blank line, then a body wrapped at about 72 columns.\n- The body explains why and any non-obvious consequence. The diff already shows what.\n- Reference issues and breaking changes in the form the project uses. If the repo uses Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix(scope):`), follow it; check `git log` to see.\n- Write the message to a file or use `-F -` with a heredoc for multi-line messages so quoting does not mangle it.\n\n## Check the diff before committing\n\n```sh\ngit diff --staged\ngit diff --staged --stat\n```\n\nRead every hunk. Look for:\n- Debug output, commented-out code, stray `TODO`s you added, editor files, `.DS_Store`.\n- Files you did not mean to touch, including mode changes and line-ending churn.\n- Secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` files, connection strings with credentials, cloud credential files.\n\n## Never commit secrets\n\n- Put secrets in environment variables or a secret store; commit a `.env.example` with placeholder values.\n- Ensure `.gitignore` covers `.env`, key files and local config before the first commit.\n- Scan before pushing when a tool is available (`gitleaks`, `git-secrets`, `trufflehog`), or at least `git diff --staged | grep -iE 'secret|token|password|api[_-]?key|BEGIN .*PRIVATE'`.\n- If a secret was committed: treat it as leaked the moment it reached any remote or shared machine. Tell the user it must be revoked and rotated first. Removing it from history (`git filter-repo`) is cleanup, not remediation, and rewrites published history, so coordinate it.\n\n## Rebasing your own work\n\nRewriting history is fine while the commits are only yours and unpublished, or on a personal branch nobody else builds on.\n\n- Update a feature branch: `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main`.\n- Tidy before review: `git commit --fixup=<sha>` then `git rebase --autosquash origin/main`. Interactive rebase needs an editor; in a non-interactive shell set `GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR=:` to accept the autosquash plan.\n- After rebasing a branch you already pushed, use `git push --force-with-lease` (add `--force-if-includes` when your git supports it). Never plain `--force`.\n- Run the tests again after every rebase. A clean rebase can still produce broken code.\n\n## Never rewrite published shared history\n\nDo not rebase, amend, reset or force-push `main`, release branches, tags, or any branch others have pulled. To undo a published commit, use `git revert <sha>`, which adds a new commit. If the user asks you to rewrite shared history, state the consequence (everyone must reset their clones) and get explicit confirmation.\n\n## Resolving conflicts\n\n1. `git status` lists conflicted files. Open each and find every `<<<<<<<` block.\n2. Understand both sides. `git log --merge -p <file>` or `git diff <base>...<other> -- <file>` shows why each side changed. Setting `merge.conflictStyle=zdiff3` shows the common ancestor too.\n3. Produce the code that keeps both intents. Taking one side wholesale (`--ours`/`--theirs`) is right only when you know the other side's change is obsolete.\n4. Check for semantic conflicts that git did not flag: a function renamed on one side and called by its old name on the other.\n5. Remove all markers (`git diff --check` and `grep -rn '^<<<<<<<\\|^>>>>>>>' .`), build, run tests, then `git add` and `git rebase --continue` or `git commit`.\n6. If it goes wrong, `git rebase --abort` or `git merge --abort` returns to the start. `git reflog` recovers lost commits.\n\n## Merge or rebase into the base\n\nFollow the project's policy (check contributing docs and recent `git log --graph`). Squash merges suit branches with messy history; rebase or merge commits suit branches whose commits each stand alone.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] On the intended branch, not the default branch unless asked.\n- [ ] `git diff --staged` read in full; no secrets, debug code or stray files.\n- [ ] Each commit is one logical change and builds; tests pass at the tip.\n- [ ] Messages have an imperative subject and a body that says why.\n- [ ] No published shared history rewritten; any force push used `--force-with-lease`.\n- [ ] Conflicts resolved with both intents kept, no markers left, tests rerun.\n- [ ] `git status` is clean or the remaining changes are explained.\n",
  "skills/orbit-plugins/SKILL.md": "---\nname: orbit-plugins\ndescription: Use when building, changing or debugging an Orbit plugin (a folder with orbit-plugin.json), or when the user wants Orbit to do something it can't do yet.\n---\n\n# Writing Orbit plugins\n\nAn Orbit plugin is a folder with `orbit-plugin.json` and a program that Orbit\nstarts and talks to with JSON-RPC 2.0, one JSON message per line on stdin and\nstdout. stderr goes to the plugin's log. Plugins live in\n`~/.orbit/plugins/<id>/`. Orbit restarts a plugin whose manifest has\n`\"dev\": true` whenever a file changes, so you can edit and test in a loop.\n\n## Before you write anything\n\n1. Read the folder: `orbit-plugin.json`, the main file (`index.ts` or\n   `main.py`), and the SDK file next to it (`orbit-plugin.ts` or\n   `orbit_plugin.py`). The SDK file is the reference for every call; don't\n   edit it.\n2. Decide what the plugin adds: tools for Claude, slash commands for the user,\n   a panel, routes, hooks, settings. Prefer the smallest set that does the job.\n3. Decide the permissions it needs (below) and nothing more.\n\n## The manifest\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"linear\",\n  \"name\": \"Linear\",\n  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n  \"description\": \"Your Linear issues in Orbit.\",\n  \"run\": \"bun index.ts\",\n  \"dev\": true,\n  \"permissions\": [\"workspaces\"],\n  \"contributes\": {\n    \"panels\": [{ \"id\": \"main\", \"title\": \"Linear\", \"entry\": \"panel/index.html\" }],\n    \"settings\": [{ \"key\": \"token\", \"label\": \"API key\", \"type\": \"secret\", \"help\": \"Linear, Settings, API\" }],\n    \"hooks\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `run` runs in the folder. `bun` and `node` work even when not installed.\n  Python needs `python3` on the computer; anything else must be installed too.\n- Permissions: `workspaces` (read, create), `agent` (send to Claude, run\n  Claude), `model` (call a model), `events` (every event), `approvals` (list,\n  answer, and needed for `before_tool` and `approval` hooks), `api` (Orbit's\n  HTTP API). Tools, commands, routes, panels, settings and storage need none.\n- Settings types: `string`, `secret` (stored encrypted), `number`, `boolean`,\n  `select` with `options`. Read them as `plugin.settings.key`.\n- Never put a token, key or password in the manifest or the code: make it a\n  `secret` setting.\n\n## TypeScript\n\n```ts\nimport { plugin } from \"./orbit-plugin\";\n\nplugin.tool(\"issues\", \"My open Linear issues\", { team: \"string?\" }, async ({ team }, ctx) => {\n  const key = plugin.settings.token as string;\n  if (!key) return { content: \"Add a Linear API key in Settings, Plugins.\", is_error: true };\n  const r = await fetch(\"https://api.linear.app/graphql\", { method: \"POST\", headers: { authorization: key, \"content-type\": \"application/json\" }, body: JSON.stringify({ query: \"{ viewer { assignedIssues(first: 20) { nodes { identifier title state { name } } } } }\" }) });\n  if (!r.ok) return { content: `Linear said ${r.status}`, is_error: true };\n  return JSON.stringify((await r.json()).data.viewer.assignedIssues.nodes);\n}, { readOnly: true });\n\nplugin.command(\"standup\", \"Write my standup from today's work\", ({ args, workspace }) => ({\n  reply: \"Asking Claude for your standup.\",\n  send: `Write my standup from today's commits and my Linear issues. ${args}`,\n}));\n\nplugin.route(\"GET\", \"/issues\", async () => ({ issues: await plugin.storage.get(\"cache\") }));\n\nplugin.start();\n```\n\nAlso: `plugin.beforeTool(({ tool, input, subject, workspace }) => ({ decision: \"deny\", reason }) or undefined)`,\n`plugin.afterTool`, `plugin.onTurnEnd`, `plugin.on(\"approval_request\", e => ...)`,\n`plugin.ready(({ settings }) => ...)`, `plugin.settingsChanged`, `plugin.stopping`,\n`await plugin.agent(prompt, { workspace?, cwd?, wait? })`,\n`await plugin.model({ prompt, system?, model? })`, `plugin.workspaces.list/get/create/send`,\n`plugin.approvals.list/answer`, `plugin.storage.get/set/delete/all`, `plugin.toast`,\n`plugin.notify`, `plugin.log`. `console.log` goes to the log, never stdout.\n\n## Python\n\n```python\nfrom orbit_plugin import plugin\n\n@plugin.tool(\"My open Linear issues\", read_only=True, team=\"string?\")\ndef issues(ctx, team=None):\n    ...\n    return \"text for Claude\"          # or {\"content\": ..., \"is_error\": True}\n\n@plugin.command(\"Write my standup\")\ndef standup(args, workspace):\n    return {\"reply\": \"Asking Claude.\", \"send\": \"Write my standup ...\"}\n\n@plugin.route(\"GET\", \"/issues\")\ndef issues_route(req):\n    return {\"issues\": plugin.get(\"cache\")}\n\nplugin.start()\n```\n\nTool functions get the inputs as keyword arguments plus `ctx` (`ctx.workspace`,\n`ctx.cwd`). Handlers may be async; inside an async handler use\n`await plugin.acall(...)` or `await plugin.aagent(...)`. Only the standard\nlibrary is available unless the user installs packages; prefer `urllib`.\n\n## Tools\n\n- The input schema can be short: `{ \"city\": \"string\", \"days\": \"number?\",\n  \"tags\": \"string[]\" }`. Claude sees the tool as `<plugin_id>__<tool>`.\n- Write the description for Claude: what it does, when to use it, what it\n  returns. One or two sentences.\n- `readOnly: true` only when it changes nothing anywhere: it then runs without\n  asking. Anything that writes, sends or deletes must not be read-only, so the\n  user's approval mode applies.\n- Return short, useful text or JSON. Errors as `{ content, is_error: true }`\n  with what to do about it.\n\n## Panels\n\nA panel is an HTML file in the folder, shown as a tab beside the conversation.\nOrbit adds `window.orbit` and its colours to the page:\n`await orbit.call(\"/issues\")` (your route), `orbit.api(\"/workspaces\")`,\n`orbit.on(\"turn_end\", fn)`, `orbit.workspace`, `orbit.send(ws, text)`,\n`orbit.toast(title, body)`. Keep it one self-contained file unless it grows.\n\n## Skills and knowledge\n\n`skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and `knowledge/*.md` in the plugin folder join the\nlibrary while the plugin is on. Add a short skill that tells Claude when and\nhow to use the plugin's tools.\n\n## Test it\n\n1. Validate the manifest is JSON and the id matches the folder.\n2. Talk to the program directly before Orbit does:\n   ```sh\n   printf '%s\\n' '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"settings\":{}}}' \\\n     '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"tool.call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"issues\",\"input\":{}}}' | bun index.ts\n   ```\n   Every request with an `id` must get exactly one answer line.\n3. Tell the user to switch it on in Settings, Plugins (new plugins start off),\n   and where to see its log. Then try the tool or command for real.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] `orbit-plugin.json` is valid, lists only the permissions used, and has no secrets.\n- [ ] Every tool has a clear description and the right `readOnly`.\n- [ ] Nothing writes to stdout except the SDK; logs go through `plugin.log` or `console.log`.\n- [ ] Errors come back as `is_error` with a next step, not a crash.\n- [ ] Tested by piping JSON-RPC lines into it, and the user knows how to switch it on.\n- [ ] `README.md` in the folder says what it does and which settings it needs.\n",
  "skills/refactoring/SKILL.md": "---\nname: refactoring\ndescription: Use when restructuring code without changing what it does: renaming, extracting or inlining functions and modules, removing duplication or dead code, or preparing code for a feature.\n---\n\n# Refactoring\n\nRefactoring changes structure and leaves behaviour exactly as it was. If behaviour changes, it is not a refactor, and it goes in a separate step.\n\n## Set the goal and the boundary\n\n- State the concrete goal in one line: \"extract the retry logic so the upload path can reuse it\", not \"clean up\".\n- List what is in scope and stop at that boundary. Note other smells you see, but do not fix them in this change.\n- Identify the public surface: exported functions, HTTP routes, CLI flags, config keys, database schema, serialized formats, event names. Changing any of these is a behaviour change for someone. Keep them stable or treat the change as a migration.\n\n## Tests first\n\n1. Run the full suite and record the result before touching anything.\n2. Check that tests actually cover the code you will move. Read them or use the coverage tool.\n3. If coverage is thin, write characterization tests first: call the code with representative inputs and assert the current outputs, including odd ones. They pin today's behaviour, bugs included.\n4. If you find a bug, write it down. Do not fix it inside the refactor; fix it before or after in its own commit, so the refactor stays provably neutral.\n5. Tests that reach into internals will break on a pure refactor. Rewrite them against the public behaviour in a first, separate step.\n\n## Small steps, always green\n\nWork in steps small enough that the suite passes after each one:\n- Make one change. Build. Run the relevant tests. Commit.\n- If a step turns red and the cause is not obvious within a few minutes, revert it (`git checkout -- <files>` for your own uncommitted edits, or `git reset --hard HEAD` only when everything uncommitted is yours) and take a smaller step.\n- For a wide change, use parallel change: add the new form, migrate callers in batches, then remove the old form. Each phase is releasable.\n\n## One kind of change per commit\n\nDo not mix in one commit:\n- Renames or moves with logic edits.\n- Formatting with anything else.\n- Refactors with behaviour changes or bug fixes.\n- Dependency upgrades with anything else.\n\nA reviewer should be able to read a rename commit as \"only names changed\" and a move as \"only locations changed\". `git diff -M --stat` and `git show --color-moved` help confirm a move is a pure move.\n\n## Renames\n\n- Use the language server or IDE rename when available; it understands scope. Otherwise search exhaustively:\n  ```sh\n  rg -n --hidden -g '!node_modules' -g '!.git' '\\bold_name\\b'\n  ```\n- Search beyond code: string references, reflection, dependency injection keys, templates, SQL, config files, docs, tests, scripts, CI files.\n- For exported names others consume, keep a deprecated alias that forwards to the new name, and remove it later.\n- Renaming a file: use `git mv` and update every import path; run the type checker or build to catch misses.\n\n## Extracting and inlining\n\n- Extract function: move a coherent block, pass in what it reads, return what it produces. Avoid passing a large context object just to save parameters.\n- Name the extracted piece for what it does, not how or where it is used.\n- Extract module: move related functions together, then fix imports. Watch for import cycles; if one appears, the boundary is wrong.\n- Inline when a wrapper adds a name but no meaning, or when an abstraction has a single caller and no prospect of a second.\n- Do not introduce a generic abstraction for two similar call sites unless they change for the same reason. Duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction.\n\n## Deleting dead code\n\nBefore deleting, prove it is unused:\n- Search for every reference, including dynamic ones: string-based dispatch, reflection, plugin registries, routes declared in config, serialization by name, exported package API.\n- Check whether external callers exist: public library exports, HTTP endpoints, CLI commands, scheduled jobs.\n- Use tooling where present (`ts-prune`, `knip`, `vulture`, `deadcode`, compiler unused warnings).\n- Delete in its own commit, so it is easy to revert. Do not comment code out; history keeps it.\n- Remove the tests, fixtures, config and docs that only served the deleted code.\n\n## Keep behaviour identical\n\nWatch for changes that look structural but are not:\n- Evaluation order and side effects when you reorder or extract expressions.\n- Short-circuiting lost when conditions are split or combined.\n- Exceptions: a different type, message or point of failure is a behaviour change if callers catch it.\n- Default arguments, mutable defaults, and shared state captured by closures.\n- Performance-sensitive paths: an extra query in a loop or a lost cache is a regression.\n- Logging, metrics and event emission that other systems consume.\n\n## When to stop\n\nStop when the stated goal is met and the suite is green. Also stop and report when:\n- The next step requires changing the public surface or behaviour.\n- Coverage is too weak to proceed safely and adding it is a larger task than the refactor.\n- The diff has grown beyond what one reviewer can read in one sitting; split the rest into follow-ups.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Goal stated; changes stay within scope.\n- [ ] Baseline suite result recorded; characterization tests added where coverage was thin.\n- [ ] Every commit builds and passes tests, and contains one kind of change.\n- [ ] No behaviour change mixed in; bugs found are reported or fixed separately.\n- [ ] Renames and deletions searched across code, config, strings, docs and scripts.\n- [ ] Public surface unchanged, or deprecations added and stated.\n- [ ] Full suite, type checker and linter pass at the end.\n- [ ] Summary lists what moved, what was deleted and anything left for later.\n",
  "skills/release/SKILL.md": "---\nname: release\ndescription: Use when asked to ship, cut, tag or publish a new release of a project: choosing the version, writing the changelog and release notes, building, publishing and verifying it.\n---\n\n# Release\n\nA release is done when a user can install the published artifact and it works, not when the tag exists. Work through the steps in order. Stop and report if any check fails; do not paper over it.\n\n## 1. Learn how this project releases\n\nBefore changing anything, read:\n\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `RELEASING.md`, `docs/` for a documented process. If one exists, follow it over this skill.\n- CI workflows (`.github/workflows/`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`) for jobs triggered by tags or releases. Many projects publish automatically on tag push; you must know before you push.\n- The package manifest(s) for where the version lives: `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, a `VERSION` file, a constant in source. There may be several.\n- Recent tags (`git tag --sort=-creatordate | head`) for the tag format (`v1.4.0` or `1.4.0`).\n- The existing `CHANGELOG.md` format.\n\nConfirm with the user before any step that is public and hard to undo: pushing a tag, publishing to a registry, creating a public release.\n\n## 2. Decide the version\n\nList the changes since the last tag:\n\n```sh\ngit log --oneline <last-tag>..HEAD\ngit diff --stat <last-tag>..HEAD\n```\n\nApply Semantic Versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) to the public API, which includes CLI flags, config keys, file formats, and documented behavior:\n\n- **MAJOR**: any backward-incompatible change. Removed or renamed options, changed defaults that alter behavior, dropped platform or runtime support.\n- **MINOR**: new backward-compatible functionality, or a deprecation.\n- **PATCH**: backward-compatible bug fixes only.\n- `0.y.z`: initial development, anything may change; by convention breaking changes bump MINOR. Once people rely on it in production, it should be `1.0.0`.\n- Pre-releases: `2.0.0-rc.1`, `2.0.0-beta.2`. They sort before `2.0.0`.\n- Never reuse or move a published version. A mistake gets a new version.\n\nWhen unsure whether a change is breaking, treat it as breaking or ask.\n\n## 3. Write the changelog for users\n\nFollow the Keep a Changelog shape:\n\n```markdown\n## [1.5.0] - 2026-10-23\n\n### Added\n- `--dry-run` flag for `sync` shows what would change without writing.\n\n### Changed\n- `sync` now skips hidden files by default. Pass `--include-hidden` for the old behavior.\n\n### Deprecated\n- `legacy_mode` config key; it will be removed in 2.0.0.\n\n### Fixed\n- Crash when the config file was empty (#412).\n\n### Security\n- Updated the YAML parser to fix an unsafe-load issue.\n```\n\n- Sections: Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, Security. Omit empty ones.\n- Newest release first. Keep an `## [Unreleased]` section at the top if the project uses one, and move its entries into the new version.\n- Dates in ISO format, `YYYY-MM-DD`.\n- Write what changed for the user and what they must do, not the commit message. \"Fix race in worker pool\" becomes \"Fixed jobs occasionally running twice under heavy load.\"\n- Every breaking change states the migration step.\n- Do not dump the git log. Leave out refactors, CI tweaks, and internal changes unless they affect users.\n- Update compare links at the bottom if the file has them.\n\n## 4. Bump, test, build\n\n1. Update the version in every place it lives. Search for the old version string to catch stragglers: `grep -rn \"1.4.2\" --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=.git`.\n2. Update lockfiles if the version is recorded there.\n3. Run the full test suite, linters, and type checks locally. All green, no skipped failures.\n4. Build the release artifacts exactly as CI does (same command, clean checkout or clean build directory).\n5. Inspect the artifact: list package contents (`npm pack --dry-run`, `tar tzf`, `unzip -l`, `python -m build` then check the wheel). Confirm no secrets, no stray large files, and that required files are present.\n6. Commit as `Release 1.5.0` (or the project's convention).\n\n## 5. Tag and publish\n\n- Create an annotated tag on the release commit: `git tag -a v1.5.0 -m \"v1.5.0\"`. Sign it if the project signs tags.\n- Push the commit, then the tag. Watch the CI release job to completion.\n- If publishing manually, publish the exact artifact you inspected.\n- Create the release on the hosting platform with release notes.\n\nRelease notes are the changelog entry plus, at the top, two or three lines on the highlights and any action required: \"Breaking: `sync` skips hidden files by default. See Changed.\" Include upgrade instructions and checksums if the project publishes binaries.\n\n## 6. Install from the published artifact and smoke test\n\nIn a clean environment (fresh directory, temp virtualenv, container), not your working tree:\n\n```sh\nnpm install -g acme-cli@1.5.0   # or pip install acme==1.5.0, or download the binary\nacme --version                  # prints 1.5.0\nacme init demo && acme sync --dry-run\n```\n\n- Check the version string matches.\n- Run the main path a new user would take, plus one thing that changed in this release.\n- For binaries, verify on each platform you can reach and check the published checksums.\n- Confirm docs or install instructions that reference the version are correct.\n\n## 7. Rollback plan\n\nKnow this before you publish:\n\n- **Registry packages**: most registries forbid reusing a version. The fix is a new patch release. Deprecate or yank the bad version (`npm deprecate`, PyPI yank, `cargo yank`) so new installs avoid it.\n- **Tags and releases**: if the tag is pushed but nothing was published, you may delete it (local and remote) with the user's approval. Once published, leave it and ship a fix.\n- **Deployed services**: identify the previous version and the exact command to redeploy it, and any data migration that cannot be reversed.\n- Write the plan down in your summary: what you would run and what users would see.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] You followed the project's own release process where one exists.\n- [ ] The version follows semver given the actual changes; breaking changes bump MAJOR (or MINOR in 0.x).\n- [ ] The version is updated everywhere; no old version string remains.\n- [ ] The changelog is written for users, dated, and gives migration steps.\n- [ ] Tests, lint, type checks, and build passed from a clean state.\n- [ ] Artifact contents were inspected before publishing.\n- [ ] Public, irreversible steps were confirmed with the user.\n- [ ] You installed the published artifact in a clean environment and the smoke test passed.\n- [ ] Release notes lead with highlights and required actions.\n- [ ] The rollback plan is written down.\n",
  "skills/research-report/SKILL.md": "---\nname: research-report\ndescription: Use when asked to research a question across several sources and write up the findings, such as a comparison, a market or technology scan, a fact check, or a briefing with citations.\n---\n\n# Research Report\n\nYour job is a report the reader can act on and verify. Every claim traces to a source they can open. The answer comes first.\n\n## 1. Frame the question\n\nBefore searching, write down:\n\n- **The main question**, in one sentence. If the request is vague (\"look into vector databases\"), restate it as a decision or a question (\"Which vector database fits a 10M-row, self-hosted, Postgres shop?\") and say so in the report.\n- **Three to seven sub-questions** whose answers together answer the main one. Example for \"Should we move CI to self-hosted runners?\": cost per build minute today vs self-hosted; maintenance effort; security exposure; build speed; who has done it and what they reported.\n- **What would change the answer.** Name the facts that would flip the conclusion. Search hardest for those.\n- **Time sensitivity.** Decide how old a source can be before it is useless. Pricing, versions, laws, and market share go stale in months. Concepts and history do not.\n\nIf the scope is ambiguous in a way that changes the work (country, budget, audience), ask one short question before you start. Otherwise state your assumption and proceed.\n\n## 2. Search wide, then deep\n\n**Wide pass.** For each sub-question, run several searches with different wording, including the terms an expert would use and the terms a critic would use (\"X problems\", \"X vs Y\", \"migrated away from X\"). Skim results; collect candidate sources. Do not read deeply yet.\n\n**Deep pass.** Open the best candidates and read the relevant sections in full. Follow links from secondary sources to the primary source they cite. Stop a sub-question when new sources only repeat what you have.\n\nSource preference, best first:\n\n1. Primary: the official documentation, specification, standard, statute, filing, dataset, changelog, or the paper itself.\n2. Direct reports from people who did the thing: engineering blogs, post-mortems, conference talks with data.\n3. Reputable secondary sources with named authors and their own reporting.\n4. Aggregators, listicles, vendor comparison pages, anonymous forum posts. Use these only to find leads, never as the sole support for a claim.\n\nTreat a vendor's claims about its own product as claims, not facts. A benchmark published by the winner needs independent confirmation.\n\n## 3. Check every source\n\nFor each source you will cite, check quickly:\n\n- **Who** wrote it and what they gain if you believe it. Read about the source from other sites, not from its own About page.\n- **When** it was published or last updated. Note the date. If a fact may have changed since, look for a newer source or flag it.\n- **What it actually says.** Quote or paraphrase the specific passage. Do not cite a page for something it only implies.\n- **Where the claim originated.** If five articles repeat one number, trace it to the original and cite that.\n\n## 4. Triangulate\n\n- A key claim (one the conclusion depends on) needs two independent sources, or one primary source.\n- When sources disagree, report the disagreement, give each side's source, and say which you find stronger and why (newer, primary, larger sample, no conflict of interest).\n- Numbers: record the unit, date, and population. \"40% faster\" means nothing without \"than what, measured how, on what workload\".\n- If you cannot verify something important, say so plainly rather than dropping it or stating it as fact.\n\n## 5. Separate known from claimed\n\nUse wording that tells the reader how solid each statement is:\n\n| Status | Wording |\n| --- | --- |\n| Verified in primary sources | \"X supports Y ([docs](link)).\" |\n| Reported, not independently verified | \"The vendor states that... ([link])\" / \"According to a 2025 survey by...\" |\n| Contested | \"Sources disagree: A reports..., B reports...\" |\n| Your inference | \"This suggests...\", \"Likely, because...\" |\n| Unknown | \"No public data found on...\" |\n\nNever upgrade a claim's status in the summary. If the body says \"the vendor states\", the summary cannot say \"X is\".\n\n## 6. Write the report\n\nUse this structure unless the user asked for another:\n\n```markdown\n# <Question as a title>\n\n**Answer:** <2 to 5 sentences. The direct answer, the main reason, and the confidence level.>\n\n## Key findings\n- <Finding, with the number or fact that supports it> ([Source](url), 2026-03)\n- ...\n\n## Evidence\n### <Sub-question 1>\n<Short paragraphs or a table. Cite inline after each claim.>\n### <Sub-question 2>\n...\n\n## Comparison (if comparing options)\n| Criterion | Option A | Option B |\n\n## Open questions and caveats\n- <What you could not verify, what may change soon, what depends on the reader's situation>\n\n## Sources\n1. <Title>, <publisher>, <date>. <url>\n```\n\nWriting rules:\n\n- Cite every factual claim with an inline Markdown link to the specific page, not the site's home page. One link per claim is enough; put it right after the claim.\n- Put dates on time-sensitive facts: \"as of September 2026\", \"pricing page checked 2026-10-23\".\n- Lead each section with its conclusion, then the support.\n- Use tables for comparisons across more than two criteria.\n- Keep the answer section free of hedging beyond one clear confidence statement (\"High confidence\", \"Moderate: based on two vendor reports and no independent benchmark\").\n- Do not pad. If a sub-question turned out not to matter, drop it or give it one line.\n- Write the report into a document (the doc editor or a Markdown file) when it runs longer than a screen; give a short summary in chat with a pointer to it.\n\n## Decision rules\n\n- If the evidence is too thin to answer, say that is the finding, then give the best partial answer and what would settle it.\n- If the user's premise is wrong (\"why is X deprecated?\" when it is not), say so first, with the source.\n- If a source is paywalled and you only saw the abstract or snippet, cite it as such and do not claim details you did not read.\n- Prefer fewer, stronger sources over many weak ones.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] The first paragraph answers the question that was asked.\n- [ ] Every factual claim has a working link to a page that actually supports it.\n- [ ] Key claims rest on a primary source or two independent ones.\n- [ ] Time-sensitive facts carry a date, and you checked none are stale.\n- [ ] Vendor and self-reported claims are labelled as claims.\n- [ ] Disagreements between sources are reported, not hidden.\n- [ ] Open questions and assumptions are listed.\n- [ ] Numbers carry units, dates, and what they compare against.\n- [ ] You reread the summary against the body: no claim got stronger on the way up.\n",
  "skills/security-review/SKILL.md": "---\nname: security-review\ndescription: Use when reviewing code, a diff or a design for security problems such as injection, broken access control, secrets exposure, SSRF, XSS or CSRF, or when asked whether something is safe to ship.\n---\n\n# Security Review\n\nFind issues an attacker can actually exploit, show how, and give a fix. A finding without a plausible attack path is a note, not a vulnerability.\n\n## Map the system first\n\n1. Identify entry points: HTTP routes, RPC handlers, CLI arguments, message consumers, file uploads, webhooks, scheduled jobs reading external data, environment and config.\n2. Identify trust boundaries: where data crosses from a less trusted party (browser, other tenant, third-party API, uploaded file, another service) into code that acts on it.\n3. Identify sensitive sinks: database queries, shell execution, filesystem paths, outbound HTTP, HTML rendering, deserializers, template engines, redirects, logging, crypto, permission checks.\n4. Trace each untrusted input from entry to sink. Note where it is validated, encoded or authorized, and where it is not.\n\nPrioritise code that handles authentication, money, personal data, file access and admin functions.\n\n## Injection\n\nAll injection has one fix: keep data out of the code channel. Validation helps; it is not the fix.\n\n- SQL: look for string concatenation or interpolation into queries, including ORDER BY, LIMIT, table and column names. Fix with parameterized queries; allowlist identifiers that cannot be parameterized.\n  ```python\n  bad:  db.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '{email}'\")\n  good: db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s\", (email,))\n  ```\n- Shell: look for `system`, `exec`, `popen`, `subprocess(..., shell=True)`, backticks, `child_process.exec`. Fix by calling the program with an argument array and no shell, and put `--` before user-supplied arguments so a value starting with `-` is not read as an option.\n- Path traversal: look for user input joined into a path. Fix by resolving the full path (realpath, following symlinks) and checking it is inside the allowed base directory; reject absolute paths and `..`; prefer generated IDs over user filenames.\n- Also check: template injection, LDAP, XPath, NoSQL operator injection (`{\"$gt\": \"\"}`), header and log injection (CR/LF), unsafe deserialization (`pickle`, Java native, YAML full loaders) of untrusted data.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\nBroken access control is the most common serious finding. Check every handler, not just the obvious ones.\n\n- Is there an authorization check on every route that reads or changes data, enforced on the server?\n- Is the object looked up scoped to the caller? `GET /invoices/:id` must check that the invoice belongs to the caller's account (IDOR).\n- Can a user set fields they should not, through mass assignment (`role`, `is_admin`, `owner_id`)?\n- Are admin and internal endpoints reachable from outside?\n- Passwords hashed with a slow, salted algorithm designed for passwords (argon2id, scrypt, bcrypt); never fast hashes. Check current OWASP guidance for parameters.\n- Session tokens random, long, rotated on login, invalidated on logout, sent only in `Secure; HttpOnly` cookies with a `SameSite` attribute.\n- Comparisons of secrets and tokens are constant time.\n- JWTs: algorithm pinned on verify, signature verified, expiry checked, `none` rejected.\n- Rate limiting on login, password reset and token endpoints.\n\n## Secrets handling\n\n- No secrets in source, tests, fixtures, client bundles, container images, or logs. Search: `rg -n -i 'api[_-]?key|secret|token|password|BEGIN .*PRIVATE KEY'`.\n- Secrets come from environment or a secret store, are scoped to least privilege, and can be rotated.\n- Error messages and logs do not echo credentials, tokens, full request bodies or personal data.\n- Anything shipped to a browser or mobile app is public.\n\n## SSRF\n\nAny feature that fetches a URL the user influences (webhooks, link previews, imports, image proxies) can be aimed at internal services and cloud metadata endpoints.\n\n- Prefer an allowlist of hosts; build the URL yourself from a validated host rather than accepting a full URL.\n- If destinations must be arbitrary: resolve the name, reject every resolved address in loopback, private, link-local, unique-local and metadata ranges, then connect to that validated IP (prevents DNS rebinding).\n- Disable automatic redirect following, or revalidate each hop.\n- Restrict schemes to `https` (and `http` if required). Reject `file:`, `gopher:` and others.\n- Network egress rules are the strongest control; recommend them.\n\n## XSS and CSRF\n\n- XSS: find every place untrusted data reaches HTML, attributes, URLs, inline scripts or styles. Look for `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `v-html`, `|safe`, `{{{ }}}`, `document.write`, and `href` from user data (blocks `javascript:` only if validated). Fix with context-aware output encoding from the framework, sanitize HTML that must be rendered with a maintained sanitizer, and recommend a strict Content Security Policy.\n- CSRF: state-changing requests must not be GET. For cookie-authenticated endpoints, check for framework CSRF protection, synchronizer or signed double-submit tokens, or Fetch Metadata (`Sec-Fetch-Site`) checks with an Origin fallback. `SameSite` cookies are defense in depth, not the only control. XSS defeats CSRF defenses.\n- CORS: `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` reflecting any origin together with credentials is a finding.\n\n## Dependency and supply chain risk\n\n- Run the ecosystem's audit (`npm audit`, `pip-audit`, `cargo audit`, `govulncheck`, `osv-scanner`) and check whether the vulnerable code path is actually reachable.\n- Lockfile present and committed; CI installs from it exactly.\n- New dependencies: maintained, widely used, name not a typo of a popular package, no install scripts doing unexpected things.\n- CI: third-party actions pinned to a commit SHA, tokens with minimum permissions, no secrets exposed to builds from forks.\n\n## Other checks worth a pass\n\nOpen redirects, file upload type and size limits, XML external entities, weak randomness for tokens, missing TLS verification (`verify=False`, `rejectUnauthorized: false`), verbose errors in production, debug endpoints left enabled.\n\n## Report each finding\n\n```\n[High] IDOR in invoice download\nWhere: api/invoices.ts:42 (GET /invoices/:id/pdf)\nExploit: logged in as any user, request /invoices/1001/pdf, then 1002, ...;\n  the handler loads by id without checking account_id, so you receive\n  other customers' invoices.\nFix: load with WHERE id = ? AND account_id = ? from the session; return 404\n  on miss. Add a test that a second account gets 404.\n```\n\n- Severity from impact and ease: Critical (remote, unauthenticated, data or code execution), High, Medium, Low, Info.\n- Give file and line, the concrete exploit steps or request, and a specific fix. Include a test that would catch a regression.\n- Separate confirmed findings from suspicions you could not verify, and say what would confirm them.\n- Do not run exploits against systems you have not been asked to test.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Entry points, trust boundaries and sinks listed.\n- [ ] Every untrusted input traced to its sinks.\n- [ ] Authorization checked on every handler, including object ownership.\n- [ ] Injection, SSRF, XSS, CSRF, secrets and dependencies each reviewed.\n- [ ] Each finding has location, severity, exploit scenario and fix.\n- [ ] Suspected versus confirmed issues stated clearly; nothing exploited without permission.\n",
  "skills/shell-scripts/SKILL.md": "---\nname: shell-scripts\ndescription: Use when writing, fixing or reviewing a shell script (bash or POSIX sh), such as an install, build, deploy, backup or cleanup script, or a one-off command that deletes or moves files.\n---\n\n# Shell Scripts\n\nWrite scripts that fail loudly, quote everything, clean up after themselves, and can be run twice safely.\n\n## Choose the shell deliberately\n\n- Use `#!/usr/bin/env bash` when you need arrays, `[[ ]]`, `pipefail` or `local`. Use `#!/bin/sh` only if you write strictly POSIX and test with `dash`.\n- macOS ships an old bash and BSD tools. If the script must run there, avoid newer bash features (associative arrays, `mapfile`, `${var,,}`) or require a newer bash explicitly, and avoid GNU-only flags (`sed -i ''` versus `sed -i`, `date -d`, `readlink -f`, `grep -P`).\n- If logic needs data structures, JSON or real error handling, write it in a proper language instead.\n\n## Header\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nIFS=$'\\n\\t'   # optional; only if you rely on it, and know it changes word splitting\n\nusage() { echo \"usage: ${0##*/} [-n] <target-dir>\" >&2; exit 2; }\ndie()   { echo \"${0##*/}: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n```\n\n## Know the limits of set -euo pipefail\n\nIt is a safety net, not error handling. It does not trigger in these cases:\n- Commands in `if`, `while`, `until` conditions, or left of `&&` / `||`. A function called in those positions runs with `-e` disabled for its whole body.\n- `local x=$(cmd)` and `export x=$(cmd)`: the builtin's status masks the failure. Declare first, then assign: `local x; x=$(cmd)`.\n- Command substitution in bash does not inherit `-e` unless `shopt -s inherit_errexit`.\n- `((i++))` returns 1 when the result is 0 and kills the script. Use `i=$((i + 1))`.\n- `pipefail` makes `cmd | head -1` fail when `cmd` gets SIGPIPE. Handle that pipeline explicitly.\n- `grep` returns 1 on no match. Write `grep pattern file || true` when no match is fine.\n- `set -u` fails on unset optional variables. Use `${VAR:-}` or `${VAR:-default}`.\n\nCheck important commands explicitly: `cmd || die \"cmd failed\"`.\n\n## Quoting\n\n- Quote every expansion: `\"$var\"`, `\"$@\"`, `\"$(cmd)\"`, `\"${arr[@]}\"`. Unquoted expansions split on whitespace and expand globs.\n- Use `\"$@\"` to pass arguments through, never `$*` or `$@` unquoted.\n- Use `[[ ]]` in bash; in POSIX `[ ]` quote both sides: `[ \"$a\" = \"$b\" ]`.\n- Put `--` before filename arguments that might start with `-`: `rm -- \"$f\"`, `grep -- \"$pat\" \"$f\"`.\n- Use `printf '%s\\n' \"$x\"` instead of `echo \"$x\"` for arbitrary data.\n\n## Arrays for argument lists\n\n```bash\nargs=(--archive --delete)\n[[ $dry_run == 1 ]] && args+=(--dry-run)\nrsync \"${args[@]}\" -- \"$src/\" \"$dest/\"\n```\n\nNever build commands in a string and run them with `eval` or unquoted expansion. Read lines safely with `while IFS= read -r line; do ...; done < file`. Iterate files with globs (`for f in \"$dir\"/*.log; do [[ -e $f ]] || continue; ...`) or `find ... -print0 | xargs -0`, never `for f in $(ls)`.\n\n## Temp files and cleanup\n\n```bash\ntmp=$(mktemp -d) || die \"mktemp failed\"\ntrap 'rm -rf -- \"$tmp\"' EXIT\n```\n\n- Always `mktemp`; never fixed names like `/tmp/out.txt` (races, symlink attacks, collisions).\n- Set the `EXIT` trap right after creating the resource. It also runs on `set -e` exits. Add `INT TERM` handling if you need to kill child processes.\n- Write output to a temp file in the same directory, then `mv` into place, so readers never see a half-written file.\n\n## Never rm -rf with an unchecked variable\n\n```bash\n: \"${target:?target is required}\"\n[[ -d $target ]] || die \"not a directory: $target\"\ncase $target in /|/home|/home/*/|\"$HOME\"|.|..) die \"refusing to delete $target\";; esac\nrm -rf -- \"${target:?}/build\"\n```\n\n- `${var:?}` aborts if the variable is empty or unset, so `rm -rf \"${dir:?}/\"*` cannot become `rm -rf /*`.\n- `cd \"$dir\" || exit 1` before deleting relative paths; a failed `cd` followed by `rm -rf *` deletes the wrong directory.\n- Prefer deleting specific known paths over globs. Print what will be deleted in dry-run mode.\n\n## Idempotence and dry runs\n\n- Running the script twice must be safe: `mkdir -p`, `ln -sfn`, `cp` only if changed, check before appending to a file (`grep -qxF \"$line\" \"$f\" || printf '%s\\n' \"$line\" >> \"$f\"`), `CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS`.\n- Detect existing state and skip with a message rather than failing or duplicating.\n- For destructive or remote-changing scripts, add `-n`/`--dry-run` that prints actions without performing them:\n  ```bash\n  run() { if [[ $dry_run == 1 ]]; then printf 'would run:'; printf ' %q' \"$@\"; echo; else \"$@\"; fi; }\n  run rm -- \"$file\"\n  ```\n- Parse options with `getopts` (or a simple `while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; case ...` loop for long options). Validate required arguments and exit 2 with usage on error.\n\n## Output and exit codes\n\n- Messages and errors to stderr; data to stdout so the script composes in pipelines.\n- Exit 0 on success, non-zero on failure, 2 for usage errors.\n- Do not hide errors with `2>/dev/null` or `|| true` unless that specific failure is expected.\n- Never put secrets on the command line (visible in `ps`) or echo them; read from environment or a file with restricted permissions. Avoid `set -x` around secrets.\n\n## Check it\n\n```bash\nshellcheck script.sh            # fix warnings; disable a rule only with a comment saying why\nbash -n script.sh               # syntax check\nshfmt -d script.sh              # formatting, if the project uses it\n```\n\nRun it once with `--dry-run`, once for real in a scratch directory, and once more to confirm idempotence. Test with paths containing spaces and with empty or missing arguments.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Shebang matches the features used; portability target known.\n- [ ] `set -euo pipefail` with its gaps handled explicitly.\n- [ ] Every expansion quoted; arrays for argument lists; no `eval`.\n- [ ] Temp files from `mktemp` with an `EXIT` trap.\n- [ ] Every `rm -rf` guarded with `${var:?}` and a path sanity check.\n- [ ] Safe to run twice; dry-run mode for destructive actions.\n- [ ] Errors to stderr, meaningful exit codes, no secrets in arguments or logs.\n- [ ] `shellcheck` clean; tested with spaces in paths and missing arguments.\n",
  "skills/testing/SKILL.md": "---\nname: testing\ndescription: Use when writing, fixing or reviewing automated tests, adding coverage for a bug or feature, choosing between unit, integration and end-to-end tests, or chasing a flaky test.\n---\n\n# Testing\n\nA test exists to protect a behaviour someone relies on. Write each test so that it fails when that behaviour breaks and passes through any refactor that keeps it.\n\n## Start by running what exists\n\n1. Find the test command: look in `package.json` scripts, `Makefile`, `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, `go.mod`, CI config (`.github/workflows/*.yml`), and the README. Use the command CI uses.\n2. Run the full suite before you change anything. Record which tests pass, fail or skip. You need this baseline to tell your breakage from existing breakage.\n3. If the suite is already red, say so and do not \"fix\" unrelated failures silently. Note them and carry on.\n4. Learn the local conventions: file layout, naming, assertion style, fixture helpers, how mocks are done. Follow them even if you would choose differently.\n\n## Decide what to test\n\nTest observable behaviour through the public interface: return values, state visible to callers, emitted events, HTTP responses, files written, errors raised. Do not test private helpers, call order of internal functions, or the exact shape of intermediate data.\n\nFor each unit of behaviour, cover:\n- The main success path with realistic input.\n- Boundaries: empty, one, many, zero, negative, maximum, off-by-one edges.\n- Invalid input and the error the caller should see (type and message that callers depend on, not the full text).\n- State transitions: what changes, and what must not change.\n- Every bug you fix: write a test that fails on the old code first, then fix.\n\nSkip tests that only restate the implementation (asserting a mock was called with what the code obviously passes it), trivial getters, and framework behaviour you do not own.\n\n## Name tests after what they protect\n\nThe name states the behaviour and the condition, so a failure reads as a bug report.\n\n```\nbad:  test_parse, test_user_2, it(\"works\")\ngood: test_parse_rejects_dates_before_1970\ngood: it(\"returns 404 when the project belongs to another user\")\ngood: TestTransfer_FailsWithoutFunds_LeavesBothBalancesUnchanged\n```\n\nOne behaviour per test. Several asserts are fine when they describe one outcome. Structure the body as arrange, act, assert, with a blank line between the parts.\n\n## Pick the level\n\n| Level | Use for | Cost |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | Pure logic, parsing, calculations, branching rules | Milliseconds, precise failures |\n| Integration | Your code against a real database, filesystem, queue or HTTP layer | Seconds, catches wiring and query bugs |\n| End-to-end | A few critical user journeys through the deployed shape of the system | Slow, brittle, highest confidence |\n\nDecision rules:\n- Default to the lowest level that can observe the behaviour.\n- If the risk is in a SQL query, a migration, a serializer or a config binding, use an integration test with the real dependency (a container, an in-memory or temp-dir instance). A mock would only test your assumption about the dependency.\n- Mock at boundaries you do not control (third-party APIs, clocks, randomness, network). Do not mock your own modules to make a test easy; that couples the test to structure.\n- Keep end-to-end tests few and focused on journeys whose failure would be an outage: sign in, checkout, save and reload.\n\n## Fixtures and test data\n\n- Build data with small factories or builders that set sane defaults, and override only the fields the test cares about. The visible overrides tell the reader what matters.\n- Each test creates what it needs and does not depend on another test having run. Order must not matter.\n- Use temp directories (`tmp_path`, `t.TempDir()`, `mkdtemp`) and unique names, never shared paths in the repo or home directory.\n- Wrap database tests in a transaction that rolls back, or truncate between tests. Do not rely on a pre-seeded shared database.\n- Keep fixtures close to the tests that use them. A global fixture file that every test imports becomes a hidden dependency web.\n- Golden or snapshot files: review every snapshot change by reading the diff. Never update snapshots in bulk to make a run green.\n\n## Control time, randomness and concurrency\n\n- Inject the clock, or use the framework's fake timers. Never `sleep` to wait for something; wait on a condition with a timeout.\n- Seed random generators and print the seed on failure.\n- Fix the timezone and locale in the test environment or in the test.\n- For async code, await the actual completion signal (promise, channel, callback). Polling with a deadline is acceptable when there is no signal.\n\n## Flaky tests\n\nA test that passes and fails on the same code is a bug, either in the test or in the product. Treat it as one.\n\n1. Reproduce: run it in a loop (`for i in $(seq 50); do <cmd> || break; done`), run it alone, run it in random order, run it in parallel. Most frameworks have flags for repeat, shuffle and seed.\n2. Classify the cause: shared state or order dependence, timing and `sleep`, real network or external service, unseeded randomness, time of day or timezone, resource leaks (ports, files, connections), concurrency in the code under test.\n3. Fix the cause. Adding retries or longer timeouts hides the problem and may hide a real race in production code.\n4. If you cannot fix it now, quarantine it explicitly (skip with a reason and an issue reference) rather than leaving it to fail randomly. Report that you did so.\n\n## When the test is wrong versus the code\n\nWhen a test fails after your change, decide before editing anything:\n- The test encoded the old, intended behaviour and you broke it: fix the code.\n- The requirement really changed: update the test and say so in your summary.\n- The test was coupled to internals that you legitimately changed: rewrite it against the behaviour.\n\nNever weaken an assertion, delete a test, or add a skip just to get green.\n\n## Running the suite\n\n- While iterating, run the narrowest set: one file or one test name (`pytest path::test`, `go test -run Name ./pkg`, `npx vitest run file`, `cargo test name`).\n- Before you finish, run the full suite plus the linter and type checker the project uses.\n- Confirm a new test can fail: break the code briefly or check it failed before the fix. A test that has never failed has not been shown to test anything.\n- Read failure output fully. The first failure is usually the cause; later ones are often fallout.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Baseline suite result recorded before your change.\n- [ ] Each new test is named after the behaviour it protects.\n- [ ] Each bug fix has a test that failed before the fix.\n- [ ] Tests use the lowest level that observes the behaviour; real dependencies where the risk lives.\n- [ ] No `sleep`, no unseeded randomness, no shared mutable state, no network to third parties.\n- [ ] Tests pass alone, together and in random order.\n- [ ] Full suite, linter and type checker pass, or pre-existing failures are reported as such.\n- [ ] No assertions weakened, no tests skipped or deleted without saying why.\n",
  "skills/web-performance/SKILL.md": "---\nname: web-performance\ndescription: Use when a web page or app feels slow or must hit Core Web Vitals: measuring LCP, INP and CLS, then fixing images, fonts, JavaScript cost, rendering and caching in the right order.\n---\n\n# Web Performance\n\n## Targets\n\nCore Web Vitals, judged at the 75th percentile of real visits, mobile and desktop separately:\n\n| Metric | Good | Poor | Measures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| LCP, Largest Contentful Paint | 2.5 s or less | over 4 s | loading of main content |\n| INP, Interaction to Next Paint | 200 ms or less | over 500 ms | responsiveness to input |\n| CLS, Cumulative Layout Shift | 0.1 or less | over 0.25 | visual stability |\n\nCheck web.dev/articles/vitals for the current metric set and thresholds before reporting against them; they do change.\n\n## Measure first\n\nNever optimise on intuition. Get a baseline, find the bottleneck, change one thing, measure again.\n\n1. Field data, if the site is public: PageSpeed Insights or the CrUX API shows what real users get. This is what counts.\n2. Lab data for diagnosis:\n   ```\n   npx lighthouse http://localhost:3000 --preset=desktop --output=json --output-path=./lh.json\n   npx lighthouse http://localhost:3000 --form-factor=mobile --throttling-method=simulate\n   ```\n   Run 3 to 5 times and use the median; single runs are noisy.\n3. Chrome DevTools Performance panel with CPU throttling (4x or 6x) and network throttling. It shows live LCP, INP and CLS and the LCP element. Record an interaction to see what blocks it.\n4. In code, the `web-vitals` library reports the metrics with attribution (which element, which interaction, which script). Use it to collect field data on sites you control.\n5. Test production builds, not dev servers. Dev builds are unminified and unrepresentative.\n\nWrite down the baseline numbers and the test conditions before changing anything.\n\n## What to fix, in order\n\nFix the metric that fails, and within it start with the largest component.\n\n### LCP\n\nBreak LCP into its parts: TTFB, resource load delay, resource load duration, element render delay. The biggest part is where the fix is.\n- TTFB high (over about 800 ms): cache HTML at a CDN or edge, fix slow server work, remove redirect chains.\n- Load delay high: the LCP resource is discovered late. Put it in the initial HTML as a real `<img src>` or `srcset` (not `data-src`, not a CSS background set by JS). Add `fetchpriority=\"high\"` to it. Never `loading=\"lazy\"` on the LCP image. If it is referenced from CSS or JS, `<link rel=\"preload\" as=\"image\" fetchpriority=\"high\">`.\n- Load duration high: serve AVIF or WebP, correct dimensions with `srcset` and `sizes`, compress, serve from a CDN.\n- Render delay high: render-blocking CSS or JS, or client-side rendering. Inline critical CSS, `defer` scripts, server-render the main content, avoid hiding content until JS runs (A/B test snippets, fade-in on hydrate).\n\n### INP\n\n- Find the slow interaction (DevTools, `web-vitals` attribution, Long Animation Frames API). Split it into input delay, processing time, presentation delay.\n- Input delay: the main thread was busy. Break up long tasks (over 50 ms), defer third-party scripts, avoid heavy work during load.\n- Processing time: do the minimum before the next paint (update the UI first), then yield and do the rest. Use `scheduler.yield()` where supported with a fallback such as `await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 0))`. Debounce input handlers. Move pure computation to a Web Worker.\n- Presentation delay: large DOM, forced synchronous layout (reading `offsetHeight` after writing styles in a loop), big re-renders. Batch reads before writes, keep the DOM small, use `content-visibility: auto` on long off-screen sections, virtualise long lists, memoise expensive renders in component frameworks.\n\n### CLS\n\n- Give every image, video and iframe `width` and `height` attributes or `aspect-ratio`.\n- Reserve space for ads, embeds, banners and late content with `min-height`. Never insert content above existing content unless in response to user input.\n- Fonts: match fallback metrics with `size-adjust`, `ascent-override` and related descriptors, or use `font-display: optional`.\n- Animate `transform` and `opacity`, not `top`, `left`, `width`, `height` or `margin`.\n- Keep pages eligible for the back/forward cache: no `unload` handlers, no `Cache-Control: no-store` on HTML unless necessary. Check in DevTools Application panel.\n\n## Assets\n\nImages:\n- Use modern formats (AVIF, WebP) with fallbacks via `<picture>` when needed.\n- Always `srcset` and `sizes`; never ship a 2000px image to a 400px slot.\n- `loading=\"lazy\"` and `decoding=\"async\"` for images below the fold only.\n\nFonts:\n- Self-host or preconnect to the font origin. Preload only the one or two fonts used above the fold, with `crossorigin`.\n- Subset to the needed character ranges, use WOFF2, prefer one variable font over many static weights.\n- `font-display: swap` or `optional`; never leave text invisible.\n\nJavaScript:\n- Measure bundle size (`npx source-map-explorer`, your bundler's analyser) and unused code (DevTools Coverage).\n- Code-split by route and load heavy features on interaction. Remove dead dependencies; replace large libraries with native APIs or smaller ones.\n- `defer` or `type=\"module\"` for scripts; `async` only for independent scripts. Load third-party tags after the main content, or through a facade (for example a static preview that loads the video player on click).\n- Prefer server rendering or static generation for content pages; hydrate only what is interactive.\n\n## Caching and delivery\n\n- Fingerprinted static assets (`app.3f9a1c.js`): `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`.\n- HTML: short or `no-cache` with revalidation (`ETag`), so deploys show up.\n- Enable Brotli or gzip for text. Serve over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3. Use a CDN.\n- `preconnect` to critical third-party origins only (two or three at most).\n- Consider Speculation Rules to prefetch or prerender likely next pages when the prediction is reliable. Check current browser support.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] Baseline and final numbers recorded under the same conditions (device, throttling, build, median of several runs).\n- [ ] The LCP element is known, discoverable in HTML, prioritised and not lazy-loaded.\n- [ ] No long task over 50 ms blocks the key interactions; INP under 200 ms in lab with CPU throttling.\n- [ ] CLS under 0.1, including after fonts and late content load and on scroll.\n- [ ] Images are sized, responsive and in modern formats; fonts are subset and preloaded only where needed.\n- [ ] Bundle size checked; nothing large added without a reason.\n- [ ] Caching headers correct for hashed assets and for HTML.\n- [ ] Every change kept only if it measurably helped.\n",
  "skills/writing-clearly/SKILL.md": "---\nname: writing-clearly\ndescription: Use when writing or editing text for people to read: emails, messages, summaries, proposals, status updates, explanations, announcements, or any draft the user wants clearer or shorter.\n---\n\n# Writing Clearly\n\nThe reader should get your point in the first two sentences and be able to act without rereading. Write for a busy person who will skim.\n\n## 1. Before you write\n\nAnswer these in a line each:\n\n- **Who reads it?** Their role, what they already know, how much time they will give it.\n- **What should they do or know afterwards?** Approve, reply by Friday, understand why the launch slipped.\n- **What is the one-sentence point?** If you cannot state it, you are not ready to write. Work it out first.\n\nIf the user gave you material (notes, a thread, a report), extract the point and the facts that support it. Do not reproduce the material's order.\n\n## 2. Lead with the point\n\nPut the conclusion, request, or decision first. Background comes after, and only as much as the reader needs.\n\nBefore:\n> Following our discussion last week, I looked into the hosting options. There are several factors to consider, including cost, reliability and support. After comparing three providers, I think we should go with Provider B.\n\nAfter:\n> I recommend Provider B for hosting. It is 30% cheaper than our current setup and is the only option with weekend support. Can you approve by Thursday so we can migrate before the renewal?\n\nPatterns:\n\n- **Request**: what you need, by when, then why.\n- **Update**: status in one line (on track, at risk, blocked), then what changed, then what you need.\n- **Proposal**: recommendation, cost and benefit, alternatives considered, decision needed.\n- **Bad news**: the news, the impact, what you are doing about it. No warm-up paragraph.\n- **Explanation**: the answer in one sentence, then how it works, then an example.\n\n## 3. Structure\n\n- One idea per paragraph. The first sentence of each paragraph states that idea; the rest supports it.\n- Keep paragraphs to about two to four sentences in emails and messages.\n- Use a list when items are parallel (steps, options, requirements). Use prose when ideas depend on each other.\n- Use headings only in documents long enough to need navigation.\n- Put asks and deadlines where they cannot be missed: first line, or bolded at the end, ideally both for long messages.\n- Number steps when order matters. Bullet them when it does not.\n\n## 4. Concrete over abstract\n\nReplace vague claims with specifics the reader can check or picture.\n\n| Abstract | Concrete |\n| --- | --- |\n| Performance improved significantly. | Page load dropped from 3.2 s to 1.1 s. |\n| There were some issues with the rollout. | 40 users could not log in for two hours on Tuesday. |\n| We should leverage synergies. | Sales can reuse the support team's FAQ for onboarding calls. |\n| In the near future | By 15 November |\n\n- Name people, systems, dates, amounts.\n- Give one example for any abstract idea.\n- Use active voice with a clear actor: \"The script deleted the backup\", not \"The backup was deleted\".\n\n## 5. Cut\n\nDelete on sight:\n\n- Throat-clearing: \"I wanted to reach out to\", \"I hope this finds you well\" (unless the relationship calls for it), \"It is worth noting that\", \"As you may know\".\n- Hedges that add nothing: \"I think maybe\", \"sort of\", \"somewhat\", \"arguably\".\n- Intensifiers: \"very\", \"really\", \"extremely\", \"incredibly\".\n- Doubled words: \"each and every\", \"first and foremost\", \"final outcome\".\n- Nominalizations: \"make a decision\" becomes \"decide\", \"conduct an analysis of\" becomes \"analyse\".\n- Restating the question back before answering it.\n- Summaries of what you just said at the end of a short message.\n\nPrefer short, common words: \"use\" (not \"utilize\"), \"help\" (not \"facilitate\"), \"about\" (not \"approximately\"), \"now\" (not \"at this point in time\").\n\n## 6. Match the reader\n\n- Expert reader: skip definitions, use the field's terms, get to specifics.\n- General reader: define any term they might not know the first time, or replace it with plain words. Use an analogy only if it is accurate.\n- Executive reader: decision and impact first, detail in an attachment or a final section.\n- Match the register of the channel: a chat message is shorter and plainer than a formal letter. Keep the user's own voice when editing their draft; fix clarity, do not impose a style.\n- Keep tone neutral and direct. Do not over-apologise or over-thank.\n\n## 7. Length\n\n- Messages and emails: aim for something readable without scrolling. If it is longer, ask whether part of it belongs in a linked document.\n- Summaries: about one tenth of the source at most, and lead with the conclusion, not the chronology.\n- If the user gives a length limit, meet it exactly.\n\n## 8. Edit pass\n\nAfter the draft, do one pass per item:\n\n1. Read only the first two sentences. Do they carry the point and any ask?\n2. Read only the first sentence of each paragraph. Does that alone tell the story?\n3. Cut every sentence that would not be missed.\n4. Replace each vague word with a number, name, or example.\n5. Check every fact, date, name, and figure against the source material.\n6. Read it as the recipient: is anything ambiguous, missing, or likely to start a back-and-forth? Answer the obvious follow-up question in advance.\n7. Check the subject line (for email) states the topic and any action: \"Approval needed by Thu: hosting switch to Provider B\".\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] The point or ask is in the first two sentences.\n- [ ] Each paragraph has one idea, stated first.\n- [ ] Deadlines, owners, and decisions are explicit.\n- [ ] Vague claims are replaced with specifics.\n- [ ] Filler, hedges, and intensifiers are gone.\n- [ ] Vocabulary and length fit the reader and the channel.\n- [ ] Every fact matches the source material.\n- [ ] Email drafts have a subject line that states the topic and action.\n",
  "skills/writing-docs/SKILL.md": "---\nname: writing-docs\ndescription: Use when asked to write or improve a README, a getting-started guide, a how-to, reference pages, or other technical documentation for a project, library, tool or API.\n---\n\n# Writing Docs\n\nGood docs get the reader from arrival to success fast, and every command in them works. Write for a reader who is busy, skimming, and about to copy-paste.\n\n## 1. Know the reader and what they need first\n\nBefore writing, answer from the code and the request:\n\n- **Who arrives here?** A first-time evaluator, a new user installing it, a daily user looking something up, a contributor.\n- **What do they need in the first screen?** Usually: what this is, why they would use it, and how to get it running.\n- **What do they already know?** Do not explain `git clone` to developers. Do explain a project-specific concept the first time it appears.\n\nRead the project before writing: the entry points, the CLI help output, config files, existing docs, tests (they show real usage), and the package manifest (name, scripts, supported runtimes).\n\n## 2. Pick the right kind of doc\n\nEach page should be one kind. Mixing them is the most common reason docs feel muddled.\n\n| Kind | Reader's need | Shape | Example title |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Tutorial | Learn by doing, first time | A guided path with a guaranteed working result | \"Build your first plugin\" |\n| How-to guide | Get a specific task done | Numbered steps toward a goal, assumes competence | \"Rotate the API keys\" |\n| Reference | Look up exact facts | Complete, consistent, structured like the code | \"CLI options\", \"Config keys\" |\n| Explanation | Understand why | Prose about design, trade-offs, concepts | \"How sync conflicts are resolved\" |\n\nRules:\n\n- Tutorials and how-tos do not stop to explain theory. Link to the explanation instead.\n- Reference does not teach. It lists every option with type, default, and a one-line meaning.\n- Explanation does not contain step lists.\n\n## 3. README structure\n\nA README is a landing page. Default order:\n\n1. **Name and one sentence** saying what it does and for whom. \"Orbit is a local workspace where...\" not \"A next-generation platform for...\".\n2. **Quickstart**: the shortest path from nothing to a visible result. Install, one command, what you should see.\n3. **Example**: one realistic usage showing the main value.\n4. **Install details**: requirements, platform notes, alternatives.\n5. **Usage / Configuration**: the common cases, then a link to full reference.\n6. **Links**: docs site, contributing, license, support.\n\nCut badges, marketing, and roadmaps unless the user asks for them.\n\n## 4. Quickstarts that actually run\n\n- Start from a clean state: state prerequisites explicitly (runtime, OS, accounts, keys).\n- Every step is a command or an action, in order, with nothing implied.\n- Show the expected output, or a short excerpt of it, after the key command so the reader knows it worked.\n- Use real values or obvious placeholders: `YOUR_API_KEY`, `<project-name>`. Say where to get each one.\n- Keep it under about ten steps. If it needs more, the product needs a setup script or the doc needs splitting.\n\nExample:\n\n````markdown\n## Quickstart\n\nRequires Node.js (the version listed under `engines` in `package.json`).\n\n```sh\nnpm install -g acme-cli\nacme init my-site\ncd my-site && acme dev\n```\n\nOpen http://localhost:3000. You should see the starter page.\n````\n\n## 5. Examples\n\n- Every non-trivial feature gets at least one example, placed next to its description.\n- Examples are complete enough to run: include imports, setup, and the call. Mark elided parts clearly with a comment.\n- Show input and output together.\n- Prefer one realistic example over three toy ones. `createUser({ email: \"ana@example.com\" })` beats `foo(bar)`.\n- Use language tags on every code block (`sh`, `ts`, `json`, `toml`) so it highlights.\n- Separate the command from its output; do not prefix commands with `$` if readers will copy them.\n\n## 6. Keep docs true\n\n- **Run every command you document**, in a fresh directory or a clean shell where possible. If you cannot run one (needs paid account, other OS), say so in your summary to the user.\n- Copy flag names, config keys, defaults, and env var names from the source code or `--help`, not from memory.\n- Check every relative link and anchor resolves to a file or heading that exists.\n- When code changes behavior, update the docs in the same change. When you notice a doc that is wrong, fix it or report it.\n- Avoid facts that rot: \"currently\", \"new in\", hard-coded version numbers in prose. Point to the changelog or use the version variable.\n\n## 7. Plain language\n\n- Use \"you\" and the imperative: \"Run `make`\", not \"The user should run `make`\".\n- Short sentences. One instruction per step.\n- Common words: \"use\" not \"utilize\", \"start\" not \"initiate\", \"so\" not \"in order to\".\n- Define a term the first time, then use exactly that term every time. Do not alternate \"workspace\", \"project\", and \"space\" for one concept.\n- Avoid \"simply\", \"just\", \"easy\", \"obviously\". They insult the reader who is stuck.\n- Put conditions before instructions: \"If you use Windows, run...\".\n- Use headings readers would search for. \"Configure a proxy\" beats \"Networking considerations\".\n- Put warnings before the step they apply to, not after.\n\n## 8. Reference pages\n\n- Generate from source where possible (CLI help, schema, type definitions) so it stays complete.\n- Order consistently: alphabetical, or by frequency of use, and say which.\n- For each item: name, type, default, required or optional, one-line description, example.\n\n```markdown\n### `timeout`\nType: integer (seconds). Default: `30`. Optional.\nMaximum time to wait for a response before failing. Example: `timeout = 120`\n```\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] The first screen says what it is, who it is for, and how to start.\n- [ ] Each page is one kind: tutorial, how-to, reference, or explanation.\n- [ ] You ran every command in a clean environment, or listed the ones you could not run.\n- [ ] Flags, keys, defaults, and paths match the current source.\n- [ ] Every example runs as written and shows its output.\n- [ ] Links and anchors resolve.\n- [ ] Placeholders are obvious and you say where to get real values.\n- [ ] No \"simply\", \"just\", \"easy\", no marketing, no stale version claims in prose.\n- [ ] Headings match what a reader would search for.\n",
  "templates/knowledge/about-me.md": "template: About me\ntemplate_description: Who you are and how you like to work, kept in front of Claude.\n---\ntitle: About me\ndescription: Who the user is, what they work on and how they like to work.\nalways: true\n---\n\n# About me\n\n- **Name and role:**\n- **What I work on:**\n- **Languages and tools I use:**\n- **How I like answers:** (short or detailed, examples first, ...)\n- **Things to always do:**\n- **Things to never do:**\n",
  "templates/knowledge/glossary.md": "template: A glossary\ntemplate_description: The words that mean something specific in your work.\n---\ntitle: Glossary\ndescription: Terms with a specific meaning in the user's work, and what they mean.\nalways: false\n---\n\n# Glossary\n\n**Term**: what it means here, and what it does not mean.\n",
  "templates/knowledge/people.md": "template: People and teams\ntemplate_description: Who is who, what they own and how to reach them.\n---\ntitle: People and teams\ndescription: Who is who in the user's work, what each person owns and how to reach them.\nalways: false\n---\n\n# People and teams\n\n| Name | Role | Owns | Reach them |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n",
  "templates/knowledge/project-brief.md": "template: A project brief\ntemplate_description: What a project is, who it is for, how it is built and what is decided.\n---\ntitle: Project brief\ndescription: What this project is, who it is for, how it is built and what has been decided.\nalways: false\n---\n\n# Project brief\n\n## What and for whom\n\n## How it is built\n\n## Decided\n\n- Decision, with the reason and the date.\n\n## Open questions\n",
  "templates/skill/review.md": "template: A review checklist\ntemplate_description: What to check when reviewing something, from most to least important.\n---\nname: my-review\ndescription: Use when reviewing ... (a pull request, a design, a text) against these standards.\n---\n\n# My review checklist\n\n## Read first\n\nWhat to read or run before judging anything.\n\n## Check, in this order\n\n1. **Correctness.** What would make it wrong for the people who use it.\n2. **Safety.** What could leak, break or be lost.\n3. **Clarity.** What will confuse the next reader.\n4. **Style.** Only what the house style asks for.\n\n## How to report\n\n- One finding per item: where, what happens, how to fix it.\n- Say what you checked and found fine, briefly.\n- Leave out taste unless asked.\n",
  "templates/skill/style-guide.md": "template: A style guide\ntemplate_description: How things should look or read: a voice, a code style, a design system.\n---\nname: my-style\ndescription: Use when writing or designing anything for ... so it matches this style.\n---\n\n# My style\n\n## Always\n\n- The rules that define the style, each with a short example.\n\n## Never\n\n- What breaks the style, each with the better alternative.\n\n## Examples\n\nGood:\n\n```\nA short example that gets it right.\n```\n\nNot like this:\n\n```\nA short example that gets it wrong, and why.\n```\n",
  "templates/skill/tool.md": "template: A tool or CLI\ntemplate_description: How to use a program, API or service you rely on, with the commands that work.\n---\nname: my-tool\ndescription: Use when working with ... (the tool), to use it the way this setup expects.\n---\n\n# Using my tool\n\n## Setup\n\nHow to check it is installed and configured, without changing anything.\n\n## Common tasks\n\n### Do the usual thing\n\n```sh\ntool command --flag value\n```\n\nWhat the output means, and what to do when it fails.\n\n## Careful\n\n- Commands that change or delete things, and how to check before running them.\n",
  "templates/skill/workflow.md": "template: A workflow\ntemplate_description: Steps for a kind of task you do often, in order, with checks.\n---\nname: my-workflow\ndescription: Use when ... (say exactly when this applies, so it is picked at the right moment)\n---\n\n# My workflow\n\n## When this applies\n\n- The situations where these steps are the right way to work.\n- Where they are not: what to do instead.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. First step, with the command or file involved.\n2. Next step. Say what to check before moving on.\n3. ...\n\n## Rules\n\n- Things that must always or never happen while doing this.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\n- [ ] The result is checked the way it will be used.\n- [ ] Anything left open is said plainly.\n"
};
